WEBVTT

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.870
Certainly to be scared has been part of the human nature

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:25.070
since its very beginnings.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:28.100
- What Alien says about us now

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.000
are those fears that we have,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.710
which may be those fears that show up when you're dreaming, the nightmares.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.990
I think he took a lot of techniques from Alfred Hitchcock,

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.990
in the sense that you don't really see everything.

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:46.390
The imagination is used, and I think that's what made it so terrifying.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:50.110
I think once you're making a movie, you better keep that pressure on,

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.820
all the way through, on anything, actually.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:58.580
But God knows on a thriller, or God knows it's a marathon of tension.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.500
I've gone to films my whole life since I was a kid.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:25.660
I mean, I loved movies and intuitively I knew that it was a thing I wanted to do more than anything.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:30.000
But the raising I had, my background,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.750
I didn't suggest that that was a practical thing to do for a living.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.590
I was a theater arts major in college.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:37.090
I wasn't a film major at UCLA.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:41.270
And I did love theater, but I realized after a while,

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.440
after producing a couple of plays, that I had to stay in New York

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:46.940
because that's where the investors are.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:50.930
And I hated the weather so much that the closest thing to theater was film.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:52.740
And I'd grown up in L.A.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.640
So I decided, the hell with that.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.320
I'm not going to live in that freezing cold.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:57.360
I'll go into film.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.000
I applied to USC, NYU.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.950
UCLA. NYU, UCLA blew me off. USC accepted. So I came out there and I was a student in the USC

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:14.730
Department of Cinema. Met up with John Carpenter. John Carpenter started to do this master's thesis

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:22.740
film, a science fiction movie. I got involved in it and it grew out of hand. It finally got too big

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:30.000
for a student movie and so in the end we ended up finding a way to put it into theaters and in

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.640
Instead of the most impressive student film ever made,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.200
we had the least impressive professional film ever made.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.000
A lot of things bothered me about "Dark Star."

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.200
We had an alien in it, which was a beach ball.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.540
It was our second try on that alien.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:52.000
I went away from "Dark Star" really wanting to do an alien that looked real.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:59.350
A couple of years down the line, I decided to do "Dark Star" as a horror movie instead

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.000
of a comedy.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.480
And that was the germ of Alien.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:09.520
When we got to talking, I realized that Dan was a cinema student at USC and was in the

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:16.400
process of making kind of a spoof on 2001, a small film called Dark Star, and he was

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:22.820
surprised to find that that was my secret love, was film, and particularly fantastic

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.360
cinema always attracted me.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.230
So basically I helped out on Dark Star.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.000
I had heard about Dark Star.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.890
Star and somebody was telling me about these two brilliant young guys and sci-fi was my love really

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:42.950
I like I also like uh suspense but I went to see Dark Star and I and I spoke to Carpenter on the

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:48.660
phone and uh I spoke to Dan on the phone but it seemed to me I just had a more rapport with Dan

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:55.900
just for the phone conversations and uh I told him I had the rights to the what became Total Recall

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:00.000
which is the original story by Phil Dick was called "We Can Remember It For You Wholesale."

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.820
So he knew the story and he said, "I love that."

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:02.820
Yeah, he said, "You got the right side?"

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:03.820
I said, "Yeah."

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.480
He said, "Okay and I got something I gotta discuss with you

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.840
that I have been working on about a year

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.280
and I need to make a break through it.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.710
Maybe you're the guy that can make me have it happen."

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.300
And that turned out to be Alien.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.440
I had this opening.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.370
I didn't know where it was gonna go.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.010
I knew I wanted to do a scary movie

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.210
on a spaceship with a small number of astronauts.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.250
Like I say, "Dark Star" is a horror movie

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.870
instead of a comedy.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:30.000
And I had this creepy opening in which astronauts

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.310
The astronauts awaken to find that their voyage has been interrupted.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:41.880
They're receiving a signal from this mysterious planetoid and alien language.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.520
They go down to investigate.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.150
They get stalled down there.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:49.150
Their ship breaks down.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.800
"I've got 29 pages written, but I don't know you.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.680
I can't trust you to leave the house for this.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.980
The story, of course, no, you have nothing written in the story."

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:57.060
I said, "No.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:58.510
I've just got the rights to the story.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:00.000
I hadn't yet tried to write it yet."

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.820
And he said, "Okay, will you sit here and just read this?"

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:05.380
And this is what I got, and I'm stuck on it, frankly,

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:06.400
but read this.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:07.540
So I sat there and I read it,

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.830
and it was very close to what we actually ended up with

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:12.920
on the screen, those first 29 pages.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:15.690
- The threat at the center of the story,

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:19.150
whatever this monster or thing or danger was gonna be,

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.290
it was not at all clear to me at this point.

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:23.090
- The first act is fantastic.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:25.880
I said, "Well, possibly, maybe I can help you with it."

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.500
And he said, "Okay, I can help you in total recall."

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:30.000
So we decided, however, that we would tackle aliens

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.660
first because Total Recall would be way more expensive because at that time we

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:40.520
envisioned Alien could be a Roger Corman movie just in one location you know

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.740
several different rooms in an old warehouse. A Chilean film member named

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:55.280
Alejandro Jodorowsky telephoned me from Paris Paris France or Paris Texas no

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.540
Paris France he had made

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:06.920
an art film called El Topo, which was very well received.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:12.030
And this man over this transatlantic phone line claimed that he had the backing and the

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:15.040
rights to make a feature film of Dune.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.040
And he loved Dan's work that he did on designing the special effects for Dark Star, and he

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:23.100
knew he had to do it at a budget with some creative people.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.530
So he hired Dan to work on it, help him with the storyline and also with the special effects.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:30.000
And Dan went off within a few weeks.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.440
He went off to Paris and France and places and was working on the Dune Project there

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:35.440
for six months.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:36.740
And I wasn't the only person there.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:43.480
He had gone to England and he had plucked up an artist who did covers for science fiction

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:46.290
books named Christopher Foss.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:54.410
And for the first time I saw somebody whose stuff I liked as much as Ron Cobb's stuff.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:56.850
He had another artist he wanted me to meet.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:00.000
He had seen this guy's work in a show that was in Paris.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:07.070
at the time took me over to to one of the really fancy hotels in Paris not the

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:12.630
one I was staying in where this artist named Hans-Rudy Giger was staying while

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:18.340
his show was on display in Paris. Giger brings out this little tinfoil I said

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:28.260
would you like some opium I said why do you take that I said I am afraid of my visions

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.240
it's only your mind.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.100
He said, that is what I'm afraid of.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:36.490
He brings out a book, an art book,

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:37.740
with his paintings in it.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:39.750
I started looking at this,

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:42.410
and he and Alejandro go into a big discussion about Dune.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:44.480
I started looking at these paintings,

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.170
and it took a minute for it to register what I was seeing.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.740
But what I was seeing seemed to be very disturbing.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.960
The oft-told story of that was Dan, of course,

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:57.240
working on the Jodorowsky Dune in Paris

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:00.000
for a year or two, and it all collapsed.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.060
And he went, you know, he went home with no money and whatever it is and slept on Ron

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:06.630
Chuset's couch.

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.050
And I was in desperation.

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:13.750
The only thing I could think to do, I had to sell a script.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:16.130
So he said, "Now, light up the board, Ron.

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:20.570
I've got this first act, and you like it," and he says, "I know, I know you have a good

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:26.330
mind from these just weeks and weeks I've worked with you before I left and what I saw

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:30.000
you did on Total Recall, which you did the short story.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.630
as you've got it. I know you can make the breakthrough for me.

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:38.990
Well, Starbeast is one of those titles that you, you know, you think of and then you,

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:44.280
you know, you throw them away. I was running through titles and they all stank, unlike

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:51.570
any of them. One morning at 3 o'clock, Ronnie's apartment, I'm typing away, writing dialogue

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:56.180
and the characters are saying the alien this and the alien that and suddenly that word

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:59.730
alien just sort of came up out of the typewriter at me.

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:04.530
I said, "Alien, it's a noun and it's an adjective."

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:07.230
They said, "Yes, that's it, I have the title."

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:10.280
- It's simple, it's one word, and no one's ever used it,

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:12.300
and it never changed from that moment on.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.620
No one ever tried, the title stuck,

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.920
and that was amazing to us, even that, just that aspect of it.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:21.560
- I went to storage, got my typewriter out,

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:23.380
took it over to Ronnie Shissette's living room,

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:26.090
and wrote it up.

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:29.020
Over the days and nights of the next three months,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.690
Ron keeping me alive by feeding me hot dogs.

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:35.970
- Dan said somehow the monster has to get on board the ship

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:37.830
and away they'll amaze everybody.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:39.150
And so I wake up in the middle of the night

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.140
and I said, "Dan, I have an idea."

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:41.980
And he said, "What?"

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:44.690
And I said, "The monster screws one of the people."

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:46.570
He says, "What, what are you talking about?"

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:48.330
I said, "He jumps in his face,

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.990
plants a tube down him, inserts his seat in him,

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:52.690
and later it comes bursting out of his stomach."

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:54.750
And Dan goes, "Oh, my God,

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:57.150
that's the most amazing thing I've ever heard.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:58.150
Nobody's ever seen anything like that."

00:09:58.150 --> 00:09:59.540
And we just sat up all night

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.590
We wrote, and in three weeks we had what I would say which is 85% of the plot, the structure,

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:11.150
we didn't write the screenplay right there, of what you saw became Alien, 85%.

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:15.290
And whether they drew from other movies, they probably did, like everybody, they drew consciously

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:19.290
or self-consciously from things like the terror from beyond space, but they actually, the

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:24.050
ingredient of this thing being incubated in a human being made the whole damn thing stand

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:25.070
out.

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:28.560
Ronnie wanted to be on board once I had written the thing.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:29.730
He saw it as a...

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:33.370
you know, a viable thing he wanted to be involved with.

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:37.550
So we made ourselves a little deal.

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:40.630
He would be on board as producer, and we started trotting it around

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:45.390
and showing it to various, you know, producing entities.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:50.900
And we got pretty close to deals in a couple of times,

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:53.460
but they fell through, well, essentially,

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:58.580
because the people on the other end were demanding too much.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.380
You know, they were trying to screw us.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.420
So we went to Roger Corman's company.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:05.860
But then I think we were dealing with Bob Remy,

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:07.100
who later became head of Universal.

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:07.920
Corman was out of town.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:09.080
And he said yes.

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:11.900
So right, that looks like a pretty amazing success story.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.380
He loved that Dan's movie was good,

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.380
even though it didn't make money.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:17.520
He knew that I co-wrote this with Dan,

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:19.540
though I'd never made any movie in my life

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:20.700
because I took my name off the other.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:22.220
And we were going to make a deal,

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:24.100
but we didn't get to signing the papers.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:27.400
Or Alien would have been made as a low-budget Corman movie.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.700
A friend of ours named Mark Haggard,

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.820
Ended up with a copy of it in his hands.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:36.240
He used to be an independent writer/director.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:38.080
Mark said, "Can I read this great script there?

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:38.880
I'll talk about you have it."

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:39.980
And Dan said, "Sure."

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:42.900
So, you know, we gave him the script, and he said,

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:44.500
"Let's just wait a few days,

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:46.580
and then we'll close our deal with Corman."

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:48.180
Well, he calls us, of course, the same night.

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:49.620
He says, "It's great. It's live, and I can get the money.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:50.820
I can get the money immediately."

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:53.180
So we looked at each other, and we said,

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:54.460
"Okay, well, look, we can't wait.

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:56.370
We don't want to blow the deal with Corman.

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:58.540
It's got to be a limited time."

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:00.000
He said, "Two weeks. Give me two weeks."

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.430
Mark Haggard knew Walter Hill,

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:05.070
a director of tough guy movies.

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:07.040
Gave it to Walter Hill.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:11.360
Walter Hill showed it to his partner, David Geiler,

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:15.390
and then they showed it to their third partner, Gordon Carroll.

00:12:15.550 --> 00:12:18.270
The three of them had just formed a new production company

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:19.830
called Brandywine Films.

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:22.970
We had offices at the then-Goldwyn Studios,

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:24.050
now Warner Hollywood,

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:27.450
and Walter's office was on the first floor, ground floor.

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:30.000
And after lunch one day,

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:36.260
He was sitting in his chair, ruminating, with the window open.

00:12:36.260 --> 00:12:43.240
And a friend of his was walking down the alleyway, and he stopped and said, "Walter."

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:48.400
This guy handed him the script to the window.

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:54.360
And he read it, and he said, "Well, maybe out of my mind," he said, "I think this, he

00:12:54.360 --> 00:13:00.000
says, but, I mean, the script is terrible, but it has one great scene in it, and read

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:01.490
What do you think?

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:06.260
I read it and I thought it was absolutely terrible.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:09.620
And I called him up and I said, "You are nuts, this is crazy, would you come to the

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:10.620
big scene?"

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:12.620
I said, "Yeah, you think it jumps out and it's on his face?"

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:13.620
He said, "That's not it."

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:14.620
I said, "Look, I'm already on page 90."

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:15.620
I said, "No."

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:17.340
He said, "Keep reading."

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:21.580
And I came to the chestburster and I called him back and said, "Well, I see what you mean?"

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:24.710
They brought it to me, I thought it was interesting.

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:29.890
A nice horror picture in outer space, I mean, what could sound better?

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:39.530
and they then completely rewrote the script from top to bottom.

00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:44.870
What I didn't understand when we made the deal with Brandywine and Guyler and Hill

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:48.880
was that Guyler and Hill wanted more than to produce this film.

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:55.360
I didn't figure this out until, in fact, after principal photography.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:14:00.000
But they targeted my script since they decided they'd

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.000
wanted all the credit for the screenplay and they didn't want me to have any right from the beginning

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:11.890
david walter took a did several drives and and it was the whole character of the film

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:17.430
not the spine of it in terms of story even though that was changed quite a bit

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:22.020
but the whole character of the film changed when walter and david took it over

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:29.320
as walter himself said in one of his speeches he gave us during pre-production he said he said

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:33.710
The greatest thing I have to bring to this project is I don't know anything about science

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:35.600
fiction and I don't like it.

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:38.780
And that was certainly reflected in the various drafts that they did.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:45.000
They took several cracks at drafts and they, from all consensuses, they were getting worse,

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.500
not better, because that wasn't their forte.

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:54.150
They did recognize it was great, but they weren't good at making it better or in fact

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:56.130
at not making it even worse.

00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:00.000
To a degree, I'm going to modify that with one exception, they made a huge contribution.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.860
In the many drafts they did, most people felt it was losing.

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:06.420
But that happens sometimes because you take a shot,

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:09.160
and then you sometimes come back to what attracted you in the first place.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.260
And they did radically. They did eight different drafts.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:12.820
For some reason, I couldn't figure out.

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:15.800
Walter Hill immediately started doing rewrites of my script.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:19.740
I remember walking into Gordon Carroll's office

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:22.100
and seeing a script on the desk and lifting up the page,

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:24.440
and it said Alien by Walter Hill.

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:26.670
Gordon walks in. I said, Gordon, what's this?

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:30.000
Gordon looked very embarrassed. He said, oh, you're not supposed to see that.

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:35.110
But they contributed one thing, which is one of the best things in the movie.

00:15:35.110 --> 00:15:40.010
And I will returnly be thankful to them, not only for starting the ball for financing,

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:41.990
but to contributing this to the movie.

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:44.690
What they invented was the robot.

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:48.790
That was not in the movie, where Ash is a robot and his head comes off.

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:52.320
That whole idea and scenario was theirs.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:53.780
We rewrote it top to bottom.

00:15:53.780 --> 00:16:00.000
I mean, all of the dialogue, all the characters, the whole sense of truck drivers in space,

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:06.550
the whole, you know, plot with the robot, with Ian Holm, with all the rest of that stuff.

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:09.040
That's all in the new script.

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:12.120
The only thing I could see he was doing was just stirring around the elements.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:16.590
First thing he did was change the names of all of the characters.

00:16:16.590 --> 00:16:21.410
One early discussion, Walter walks into the office, he's there at 20th Century Fox, and

00:16:21.410 --> 00:16:27.320
he said, "I hate all those names in your script."

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:28.320
He hates all the names.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:30.000
I mean, how do you hate a name?

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:32.880
"Oh, I hate all those names."

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:34.330
So he changed them all.

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:36.000
Of course, he didn't hate them at all.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.040
But he had the naive idea that if he changed

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:42.280
all of the characters' names, this would count somehow

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:44.640
as being a substantial rewrite of the material.

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:47.880
- With a story that's as often told as this kind of story is,

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:51.120
you have to do it in a special way in order for it to be special.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:54.360
I mean, that was our argument with, you know,

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:57.060
about the movie in general with Fox all the time,

00:16:57.060 --> 00:17:00.000
and I think they agreed, which was that you had to

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.920
treat this kind of B-monster movie as though it were an A-movie.

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:10.800
That script did not excite Fox enough for them to say, "We want to go ahead and we want

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:12.390
to make it."

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:19.700
You must be remembered that this was pre-Star Wars and we were confident, although we had

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:25.730
other places we could set the film up, we were confident that Fox would ultimately,

00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:29.170
with another rewrite and whatever, would ultimately do it.

00:17:29.170 --> 00:17:30.000
We certainly hope so.

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:37.030
And when Star Wars came out and was the extraordinary hit that it was, then suddenly science fiction

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:40.190
became the hot genre.

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:44.500
I don't know that there was consensus one way or the other about science fiction.

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:50.940
I mean since I was the responsible party for making Star Wars and it was such a hit, concerns

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:54.310
about science fiction went away very quickly.

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:58.800
They wanted to follow through on Star Wars and they wanted to follow through fast and

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:00.000
the only space ships

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.540
they had sitting on their desk, it was Alien.

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:04.970
So they green-lighted it, wham.

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:25.800
Alien thinking I was going to direct it.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:28.920
That's one of the reasons that I hadn't developed the characters.

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:35.170
I mean, developing the alien itself was such a complex problem

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:39.380
that I thought, well, I'll put the characters off for a later draft.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:43.080
It went, as most things do, through a period of trying to get a package,

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:46.400
but when you're going with the studios and they offer top dollar,

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:50.190
they went to everybody, every top A-level actor and director.

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:53.590
We'd ask Walter in the first place if he wanted to direct it,

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:58.650
and since he was part of the package...

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:00.000
Walter was originally going to do it.

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:07.940
and then he uh um decided that he did not have the temperament for special effects

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:15.090
apparently gordon talked to robert aldrich didn't like what aldrich had to say he asked

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:19.840
aldrich how he would handle the face hugging creature aldrich said oh no problem i'll just

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:24.590
go to the market and get a liver and throw it on the guy's face gordon didn't want to hear that

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:30.000
i was convinced that it that it wasn't a small picture that that the way to do it was to ski

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:39.430
it up and to do it with whatever sense of craft and design and to make it an A-picture.

00:19:39.430 --> 00:19:44.620
Peter Gates, the studio, wanted. We weren't crazy about that idea, but I think he didn't

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:51.850
want to do it finally. I talked to Jack Clayton about it, and he didn't want to do it.

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:58.160
And everybody said no, because they said, "We had no credibility." And, of course, directors

00:19:58.160 --> 00:20:00.000
would make credibility, but...

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:04.920
almost none of them wanted to do it they thought it was a stupid monster movie a dumb thing with

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:09.100
something coming out of your chest like a japanese horror movie and none of them had the vision none

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:14.480
of the a-level directors we went to had the vision to see it you needed to have a director

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:20.800
director that was going to give it some kind of real other than traditional science fiction look

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:25.590
um you had the real actors in it you had to have you know all that

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:30.000
and never let on that it was a b-monster movie but make it deliver on that at the

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.900
that level.

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:37.300
In those days, I think between The Duelist, which had been something that was completely

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:43.880
self-driven by myself and my pocketbook, because I found the writer, found the story, paid

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:46.990
for the script, got no fee and was the completion bond.

00:20:46.990 --> 00:20:49.770
So that was my first experience in the movie business.

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:57.100
I saw The Duelists at Cannes and talked to Sandy Lieberson, who knew really pretty well,

00:20:57.100 --> 00:21:00.000
Sandy was head of Fox in Europe.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.210
we kind of presented him as the candidate.

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:05.400
- Walter Hill and I went to screen the picture

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:08.540
that he had done and The Duelists.

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:11.880
We were absolutely stunned by his control of the picture,

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:13.460
his mounting of the film.

00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:15.600
It really is an extraordinary film,

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:18.920
a lot of depth richness.

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:22.640
- All of a sudden, they said that they had met

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:24.740
with a director, none of us had heard of,

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:27.440
named Ridley Scott, who was a director

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:28.740
of commercials from England.

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:38.840
I said what has he done? Well, he did a film called The Duelists, and they screened it for us.

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:51.190
Very nice looking picture. I couldn't see what it had to do with science fiction, but they weren't looking at it from that perspective, and they may have had a point.

00:21:51.570 --> 00:22:00.000
Someone had seen The Duelists at Cannes, which got a prize at Cannes, and out of the blue came this offer.

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:07.730
I think it must have been within about six months actually and I read the script and

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:11.780
I think within 26 hours I was standing in Hollywood.

00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:13.220
I read the script and I said I'll do it.

00:22:13.220 --> 00:22:14.490
They said you will?

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:15.490
I said yeah.

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:16.600
So I was flown out.

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:19.360
So I suddenly started to feel the Hollywood machine kicking in.

00:22:19.360 --> 00:22:23.230
I think it is really clever casting in actual fact, the fact that somebody who's obviously

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:30.000
got a good eye and also would give a completely different quality to what could have been a

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:32.760
Maybe an exciting, but nonetheless a B movie.

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:35.600
That first encounter was good,

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:37.840
and then I went back and said,

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:39.880
what I have to do is storyboard the movie.

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:43.210
So with great excitement and glee,

00:22:43.430 --> 00:22:44.730
I storyboarded the movie in London,

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:47.730
which actually took me three and a half weeks to work day and night.

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:51.460
Went back with the boards, and the budget doubled,

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:55.730
which assures the power of a well-thought-through storyboard

00:22:55.730 --> 00:22:59.860
because we went from 4.2 to 8.2.

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:08.390
or something like that because they suddenly saw a way to do the film which was you know not doing

00:23:08.390 --> 00:23:14.600
it a bunch of sets it looked like a you know painted cardboard boxes with a dodgy man in a

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:22.540
rubber suit running around um as the beast but in fact um i'd put a statement into it i had a you

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:30.000
know i don't like the word but had a vision of what to do with the spacecraft and what to do with the

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:37.240
suits and I was fully influenced at that moment mostly by 2001 actually. Oh and Star Wars I thought

00:23:37.240 --> 00:23:42.070
Star Wars was that first Star Wars was formidable but that was the fairy story you see Star Wars

00:23:42.070 --> 00:23:47.120
is the fairy story. I was going to do the Texas Chainsaw Massacre of science fictions which I

00:23:47.120 --> 00:23:51.400
still think Texas is one of the most formidable. If you're going to do a horror movie there it is.

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:56.460
Being a horror film and a sci-fi fanatic I kept sort of saying well this is a film you've got to

00:23:56.460 --> 00:24:00.000
because he hadn't really seen many of those things and I think so I

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:04.240
was trying to sort of in my own naive way educate him as to well this is this is a sci-fi great

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:08.240
ridley you've got to see this you know you've got to see it the terror from beyond space or

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:11.840
all that sort of thing and ridley sort of looked at him as sort of an absolute horror because he

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:17.370
wasn't excited by any of them i think the only thing he was excited by was texas chainsaw massacre

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:25.370
actually i always felt it was a pretty straightforward um action thriller the old

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:30.000
dark house basically and uh we're going to watch them each get taken out by this

00:24:30.890 --> 00:24:36.830
terrible beast that's as B movies you can get. Whoever directed this movie you know he's not

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:42.290
going to quit on a monster until he has it that you can believe it that it'll look right and I

00:24:42.290 --> 00:24:45.820
thought that was the single most important thing you could have for this movie. The thing that

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:50.300
really worried me most of all because I figured I could get all the environment right all the

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:56.160
costumes right you can get the casting right um but who was this beast what was this beast going

00:24:56.160 --> 00:25:00.000
to be and that's what we started talking you know

00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:07.250
great detail about Giger. Ridley did one thing which enamored him to me. First time I met

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:16.380
him, I shoved Giger's art book into his hands. And I saw the Giger illustrations of that one

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:20.680
in Necronomicon, which I thought, that's it, that's no question, that's it. I talked at

00:25:20.680 --> 00:25:24.820
length of Dan O'Bannon on this, and he said, what do you think? I said, that's it. He said,

00:25:24.820 --> 00:25:30.000
that's right. And the idea that came to me, looking at Giger's painting,

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:35.720
was, you know, if somebody could get this guy to design a monster movie,

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:39.310
you'd have something completely original no one had ever seen before.

00:25:39.620 --> 00:25:43.270
But Fox hated Kieger's work because they said,

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:49.750
people stay away in droves, this is so ghastly, it's too much, it's too much, it's repulsive.

00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:52.090
And we said, no, it's done so beautifully,

00:25:52.310 --> 00:25:55.170
it all depends on the way the director utilizes it.

00:25:56.710 --> 00:26:00.000
The first question you ask, what is the alien going to look like?

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:02.580
Is it going to be a guy in a rubber suit with fins?

00:26:03.730 --> 00:26:09.300
And Dan opened Necronomicon, Geeker's book,

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:12.560
and we were just stunned.

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:14.060
Same thing with Ridley.

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:17.890
The first second that Ridley saw Geeker's work,

00:26:17.950 --> 00:26:20.830
he knew that the biggest single design problem,

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:23.940
maybe the biggest problem in the film, had been solved.

00:26:24.290 --> 00:26:25.680
I just thought it was stunning,

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:27.880
and I just stuck to my gun, saying,

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:30.000
no, no, that's it, and they said,

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:32.880
but let's bring him in and talk some concepts.

00:26:33.020 --> 00:26:35.060
I said, no, you can't do that.

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:35.820
That's it.

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:37.360
We want to be shooting by so-and-so.

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:38.340
That's it.

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:41.400
Just to get this right is going to be incredibly difficult.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:44.030
This is beautiful, not just threatening,

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:47.090
and also has a very sexual connotation.

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:52.440
So in everything, it's like a rather beautiful humanoid, biomechanoid insect.

00:26:53.100 --> 00:27:00.000
So then I flew to Zurich because old Giga will not get on a jet.

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:04.460
terrified of flying I think he's probably still terrified of flying and I met him at

00:27:04.460 --> 00:27:11.120
his house and he found it to be very nice and very sweet and very articulate

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:17.170
very specific and and he's started going down this thing and I'll redesign I said

00:27:17.170 --> 00:27:21.220
no so we went through the same conversation again I said you don't need

00:27:21.220 --> 00:27:30.000
to you've got five other things to design right through from egg facehugger chestburn

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:44.660
I didn't want to have eyes because it makes the monster more dangerous, it's more scary,

00:27:44.660 --> 00:27:50.130
and because you never know where the monster is looking.

00:27:50.130 --> 00:28:00.000
This is the painting, the old alien, that Ridley Scott wanted to have for his film, and that

00:28:00.250 --> 00:28:05.490
called Necronome number four, and I did it in 1976.

00:28:09.080 --> 00:28:12.340
The only thing in the alien is,

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:16.310
the difference is that they have kind of sunglasses.

00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:20.870
Now we have this kind of shell,

00:28:20.870 --> 00:28:26.400
and here the tail, it's different, and yeah, also.

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:30.000
The other things like these tubes and this is like,

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:36.370
First, it was only a short time I should draw the monster.

00:28:36.370 --> 00:28:41.310
I did this here in Zurich, and then I had to come.

00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:42.950
Then I haven't heard anything.

00:28:42.950 --> 00:28:45.230
And then I had to come to England.

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:49.940
So he said, "Please come over for three or four weeks."

00:28:49.940 --> 00:28:54.510
And then at the end, I was about five months there, I think.

00:28:54.510 --> 00:28:56.860
All together, seven months.

00:28:56.860 --> 00:29:00.000
Ridley wants that I do the three monsters.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:04.890
That was the facehucker, the chestburster, and the big alien.

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:15.030
And then later on, I could do the rest of the surrounding of the alien.

00:29:15.030 --> 00:29:20.700
I was using Dune as my model of how to design a science fiction movie, the idea of getting

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:23.560
great artists together in a room and having them all work.

00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:29.790
And we were doing that on Alien, always me in charge, instead of Jodorowsky.

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:33.640
And we had the walls full of these dazzling designs for everything in the movie.

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:37.090
He had a kind of a wish list of people he wanted to work on this film.

00:29:37.430 --> 00:29:44.110
One was Hans Rudi Giger, myself, and Chris Foss, the British science fiction illustrator.

00:29:44.610 --> 00:29:48.430
And he always had this dream project that all three of us would work on this one film.

00:29:48.430 --> 00:29:50.330
And he got his way.

00:29:50.650 --> 00:29:55.490
And even before there was a director, Chris and I had a series of little art department

00:29:55.490 --> 00:30:00.000
above an old soundstage on Fox for a long, long time.

00:30:00.060 --> 00:30:03.510
drawing and drawing and meeting directors

00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:05.250
as they would come in and look at the drawings

00:30:05.250 --> 00:30:07.110
and try to find out who was going to do what,

00:30:07.110 --> 00:30:09.410
when and where, and if it would ever go.

00:30:09.410 --> 00:30:11.150
- I knew Ron as a political cartoonist

00:30:11.150 --> 00:30:13.090
from the Los Angeles Free Press.

00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:15.010
Everybody loved his cartoons.

00:30:15.010 --> 00:30:17.250
And I had the gall to actually call him on the phone

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:20.040
and go visit him in his apartment in Westwood.

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:21.990
We became friends that way.

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:23.530
And I discovered something that at that time

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:25.130
was not known about him,

00:30:25.130 --> 00:30:27.470
that he did science fictional paintings.

00:30:27.470 --> 00:30:29.320
And they were incredible.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:34.520
Dan came to me with the script, the original script that he and Ron Chassette had written

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:42.340
for Alien, and had me read it, and it was quite amazing.

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:47.760
And he wondered if I could help him do some visualizations, a series of paintings that

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:55.900
he could send around with the script, and I did, I did, oh, quite a few.

00:30:55.900 --> 00:31:00.000
But we were still groping as to what the thing should look like, but I was very, very

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:07.150
in astronautics and spaceflight and aerospace design and ideas of the future

00:31:07.150 --> 00:31:10.200
and also science fiction as a kind of future mythology.

00:31:10.200 --> 00:31:14.120
So all this just clicked with me and I was very, very excited about it.

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:18.170
We all contributed all kinds of things for the different versions of the script

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:21.990
and Chris Voss just turned out tons and tons of exterior drawings

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:23.990
of what the ship should look like.

00:31:23.990 --> 00:31:27.860
We kind of differed in that he was very metaphorical

00:31:27.860 --> 00:31:28.430
and

00:31:28.430 --> 00:31:29.710
sort of

00:31:29.710 --> 00:31:30.000
uh

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:36.610
fantastic. And I was very interested in tapping the idea of what things could look like if they

00:31:36.610 --> 00:31:42.310
were drawn from a more realistic consideration of what a spaceship should be without going NASA,

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:49.490
create the sense of confined space, spaces at a premium inside, that the idea that it was with

00:31:49.490 --> 00:31:53.510
all the pressure doors, that it was pressurized, all those things I thought would contribute to

00:31:53.510 --> 00:32:00.000
the tension of the film. And they weren't particularly there in a lot of the early versions and

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:08.060
visions of what it should look like so I had to do a lot of persuading. Ron Cobb was going to do

00:32:08.060 --> 00:32:12.160
the monster and he had some drawings and all the rest of that stuff and Ron's a real talented guy

00:32:12.160 --> 00:32:23.990
and ended up doing a lot of this the ship but his monster was not too hot. He kind of designed the

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:30.000
sort of spaceship interiors and thing I think the sort of Nostroma and the Narcissus he did those kind of designs

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.760
because he had... I mean, he's rather like Sid Mead was to Blade Runner.

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:40.360
He just had that expert. They were founded in logic, you know, in reality.

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:43.570
This guy thinks like something's engineered.

00:32:43.980 --> 00:32:47.480
He's got a mind that he designed something that really seems like it'll work,

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:51.520
a functioning model. He's so believable. He can make them so accurate.

00:32:52.220 --> 00:32:54.920
I was more concerned with the Nostromo,

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:57.810
because I knew that was going to be my ultimate contribution would be

00:32:57.810 --> 00:33:00.000
to bring the Nostromo to life.

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:05.430
When finally we would get Giger on board and not just having him mail things in, he would

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:07.670
be involved with the alien planet and the alien.

00:33:07.670 --> 00:33:10.650
We all knew that that's what Dan wanted, you know.

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:14.270
Chris Voss was to sort of take care of the exterior of the ship and I was going to do

00:33:14.270 --> 00:33:15.910
the interior sets.

00:33:15.910 --> 00:33:22.330
There would be a production designer, but Dan wanted all our inputs to be very, very

00:33:22.330 --> 00:33:23.330
much noted.

00:33:23.330 --> 00:33:30.000
I used to be very into the metal Herlon, the heavy metal comics from France and all the

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:31.220
and people like that

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:33.670
and I think I'd managed to

00:33:33.670 --> 00:33:36.000
infect Ridley with that enthusiasm

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:37.690
etc. He could treat

00:33:37.690 --> 00:33:39.690
sci-fi in the same way that he treated

00:33:39.690 --> 00:33:41.710
history, you know, like the duelists

00:33:41.710 --> 00:33:43.350
before that he was

00:33:43.350 --> 00:33:45.870
the sort of, he was the

00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:47.870
expert at everything, he just, he put a different

00:33:47.870 --> 00:33:49.970
slant on shooting historical

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:51.820
things, he just

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:53.810
because, I guess

00:33:53.810 --> 00:33:55.070
it was his eye for detail really

00:33:55.070 --> 00:33:57.410
I was steeped in heavy metal

00:33:57.410 --> 00:34:00.000
comics, the

00:34:00.190 --> 00:34:06.660
time like Metal Hurlant and and you know a lot of Mobius's stuff actually and I

00:34:06.660 --> 00:34:10.260
was very impressed by that and I just that in those days trying to wonder how

00:34:10.260 --> 00:34:14.990
do I apply this to movies you know how do I apply this kind of really fresh and

00:34:14.990 --> 00:34:23.120
beautifully visual thinking to film and here it was you need a hook when you're

00:34:23.120 --> 00:34:30.000
starting something and my hook was my boards I drew the boards drew the corridors drew

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:35.420
the spacesuits. When you're drawing boards and corridors and spacesuits, you're also

00:34:35.420 --> 00:34:40.750
sucking yourself into story, because I'm literally doing it, you know, chapter by chapter, scene

00:34:40.750 --> 00:34:45.670
by scene. You get ideas as you're drawing them, and the ideas fall into place not just

00:34:45.670 --> 00:34:51.490
as ideas, but as technological possibilities. And when that first idea comes, I tend to

00:34:51.490 --> 00:34:52.530
go with it and follow it.

00:35:09.440 --> 00:35:11.670
There were only seven or eight people in the whole movie.

00:35:11.670 --> 00:35:13.490
So Ridley had said to me one day, he said,

00:35:13.490 --> 00:35:15.860
I'm gonna cast every other role

00:35:15.860 --> 00:35:18.710
in the most brilliant actor I can

00:35:18.710 --> 00:35:20.150
that doesn't need help from me

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:23.360
because I'm gonna concentrate on the visual style.

00:35:32.590 --> 00:35:39.940
We had wanted not to use a classic star for the film.

00:35:39.940 --> 00:35:44.780
When you see, you see and talk and talk and people read and read and read, there's always

00:35:44.780 --> 00:35:48.890
one that suddenly goes bang, that's the one.

00:35:48.890 --> 00:35:55.100
Ridley Scott had asked me to cast his first film, which was The Duelists, and we'd worked

00:35:55.100 --> 00:36:00.000
together really well and it was very enjoyable, so totally different from Aliens, it was the

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:05.420
napoleonic wars full of character actors first of all I felt I wanted to see

00:36:05.420 --> 00:36:10.500
every American every actor in the United States in the directory okay so I think

00:36:10.500 --> 00:36:15.840
we did see every actor in the United States in Los Angeles New York was about

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:20.720
as far removed as you could get from his first film because the script was very

00:36:20.720 --> 00:36:30.000
very linear it was very very few stage directions you could not tell who was a man and who

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:31.960
was a woman. I sensed deliberately.

00:36:32.470 --> 00:36:33.930
We wrote it to be all men.

00:36:35.030 --> 00:36:36.390
So I'm getting everybody their due.

00:36:36.610 --> 00:36:38.590
There's a lot of people that had to make some good

00:36:38.590 --> 00:36:40.730
guesses here. I think maybe that's the only way

00:36:40.730 --> 00:36:42.510
most of the really great movies

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:44.630
get made. But there's somebody in each

00:36:44.630 --> 00:36:46.210
spot that's doing something amazing

00:36:46.210 --> 00:36:48.470
and you don't think of it and wow, it makes

00:36:48.470 --> 00:36:50.230
it, it's part of what makes it magic.

00:36:50.470 --> 00:36:52.120
David Guyler and Walter who

00:36:52.120 --> 00:36:53.840
sort of took over the, you know,

00:36:54.140 --> 00:36:55.920
the writing from

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:58.180
Ron and from Dan.

00:36:58.340 --> 00:37:00.000
I think they came up

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:01.120
I'm not sure whether it was David or not,

00:37:01.120 --> 00:37:03.810
the idea of sort of to try and break the mold

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:04.930
of making Ripley a woman

00:37:04.930 --> 00:37:09.420
and of kind of making it more,

00:37:09.560 --> 00:37:11.340
I mean, the truck drivers in space analogy

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:13.040
I think is a pretty good one, you know,

00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:14.160
and that appealed to Ridley

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:16.240
because, you know, the thing that didn't appeal to him

00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:17.640
as an art director was sort of, you know,

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:19.840
shiny flash Gordon surfaces and things like that.

00:37:19.900 --> 00:37:22.660
He wanted the thing to be just like a battered old truck

00:37:22.660 --> 00:37:25.360
where there was bits of tape over cracks

00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:27.800
in the chairs they sat in and things like that.

00:37:28.140 --> 00:37:30.000
And we realized that studios

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:34.380
might resist it even though we went to Corman first we thought that Fox might

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:39.300
not make the movie unless we managed to make it as commercial as possible so we

00:37:39.300 --> 00:37:43.310
had a little asterisk please note any one of the crew members or let's say two

00:37:43.310 --> 00:37:49.250
could be females if you want to have it go for a wider broader appeal to all

00:37:49.250 --> 00:37:54.820
truth we did not in any way remotely conceive the idea that the lead Ripley

00:37:54.820 --> 00:38:00.000
would be a woman we were looking it over and Walter and I sort of thought here's this one

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:04.880
character not too interesting you know and you know this studio was just made

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:10.490
I hate to say this but for very cynical reasons this studio is making Julia and

00:38:10.490 --> 00:38:14.390
turning point and they really believe in the return of the woman's movie but we

00:38:14.390 --> 00:38:20.650
get a lot of points you know if we make if we turn this character into a woman

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:24.800
and it'll be it'll just make the character more interesting she wasn't

00:38:24.800 --> 00:38:29.860
originally in the original script originally the protagonist was a male

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:32.830
So that he said was one day, can Ripley be a woman?

00:38:33.620 --> 00:38:36.420
And we looked at each other, we said, yeah, but it seems kind of funny.

00:38:36.620 --> 00:38:40.920
I mean, they've never done that, a woman in a space horror movie as the leading character.

00:38:41.330 --> 00:38:45.050
And he said, well, I have a feeling that's going to make it unique.

00:38:45.460 --> 00:38:52.580
And we started going through lists of actresses and who was right for the role.

00:38:52.580 --> 00:38:58.530
You have to remember Sigourney Weaver in those days was not a big star, and she tested for the part.

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:00.000
I got this.

00:39:00.510 --> 00:39:08.260
audition. They'd sent me the script, which of course, not knowing the Ramboldi designs,

00:39:08.260 --> 00:39:14.570
I pictured this big yellow blob of gelatin chasing these people, and nothing as elegant

00:39:14.570 --> 00:39:19.900
as what it turned out to be. And I actually went to the wrong place, and I called my agent

00:39:19.900 --> 00:39:30.000
and I said, you know, it was like Friday afternoon, I said, I don't know, you know, I mean, the

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:35.750
last go in New York, uh, she'd come recommended because of a play that she'd been doing on

00:39:35.750 --> 00:39:42.800
Broadway. She was known as a, a real comer on Broadway, right? And, um, I didn't see the play,

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:47.560
I just met her. As I remember, we were in the Regency Hotel at the time. Sigourney, for some

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:53.200
reason, had gone to another hotel. We did not know this. And she was due at whatever, 2.30,

00:39:53.200 --> 00:40:00.000
and, uh, 2.30 arrives, and 2.45 arrives, and 10 minutes to 3 arrives. And, uh,

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:07.820
And then we hear, I can't say running feet in the corridor, but we hear fast-paced feet

00:40:07.820 --> 00:40:11.680
coming toward the door and slowing down and composing itself.

00:40:11.680 --> 00:40:16.250
And the bell rings and Mary opens the door.

00:40:16.250 --> 00:40:22.660
And Ripley was standing there, this extraordinary-looking woman, tall, commanding presence.

00:40:22.660 --> 00:40:30.000
And I was wearing these very high hooker boots, and I looked about seven feet tall.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:37.030
situation projecting composure but you knew that just an eighth of an inch

00:40:37.030 --> 00:40:42.700
behind that composure was a very nervous actress who are very tense actress and

00:40:42.700 --> 00:40:49.050
that was exactly right for what we knew David and I knew immediately I was very

00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:54.260
impressed by her you know height and she's a very impressive character very

00:40:54.260 --> 00:41:00.000
intelligent and seemed to make sense to me so I expressed

00:41:00.030 --> 00:41:04.090
sister laddie who said yeah but you got a test and I said but I'm three weeks from

00:41:04.090 --> 00:41:09.000
principal photography and we got to do what was basically a run-through of the

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:14.630
movie with the real kind of set and you know I was afraid it would be like me

00:41:14.630 --> 00:41:21.150
and a potted palm you know and I'd have to go but he gave me this whole run

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:23.390
through which as an actor I really appreciated

00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:30.000
Sigoni was the last one to get cast the sets the the test I shot in were the set

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:30.960
I was building.

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:33.080
That was the Nostromon.

00:41:33.080 --> 00:41:34.250
We didn't write a lot together.

00:41:34.250 --> 00:41:37.170
I was actually building, so it was scary.

00:41:37.170 --> 00:41:40.140
What I didn't like about it that much was my performance,

00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:44.880
because I think I was trying to be tough instead of just...

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:49.650
What I discovered in the course of this, which was my first film, basically,

00:41:49.650 --> 00:41:51.830
was you don't try to...

00:41:51.830 --> 00:41:57.980
You just play each scene for what it is instead of trying to work on an arc.

00:41:57.980 --> 00:42:00.000
The people aren't consistent.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:02.000
they would be different in a love scene

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:04.320
than they would be as they're fighting an alien

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:06.510
they screen tested many people

00:42:06.510 --> 00:42:08.090
but she was dynamite

00:42:08.090 --> 00:42:10.330
you saw it right there in the screen test

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:11.670
as you saw later in the movie

00:42:11.670 --> 00:42:12.710
this was her very first film

00:42:12.710 --> 00:42:14.570
it's all behind the eyes

00:42:14.570 --> 00:42:19.890
my intuition always in casting

00:42:19.890 --> 00:42:21.600
is from the second they walked through the door

00:42:21.600 --> 00:42:23.220
when I see somebody walk through that door

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:26.080
I knew somehow this was her

00:42:32.910 --> 00:42:37.600
Then there was the second woman, Lambert,

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:41.340
but it was a difficult one to finally arrive

00:42:41.340 --> 00:42:42.890
at Veronica Cartwright.

00:42:42.890 --> 00:42:45.130
I can't remember the film she'd been in before,

00:42:45.130 --> 00:42:48.420
but she played a way out weird sort of character.

00:42:48.420 --> 00:42:51.620
And she had a particular intensity

00:42:51.620 --> 00:42:54.050
and unconventional looks,

00:42:54.050 --> 00:42:56.610
which made her very, very right for it.

00:42:56.610 --> 00:42:59.600
- I went on an interview in,

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:08.420
Angeles and I met with the casting people there. Originally I had read for

00:43:08.420 --> 00:43:17.930
Ripley and I was called back and I read again for Ripley with Ridley there. Then

00:43:17.930 --> 00:43:24.920
I was due to go over to England so I had gone over for vacation so I talked to my

00:43:24.920 --> 00:43:30.000
agent before I left and I said are they still casting for Alien? I mean I might as well go see him again or

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:32.260
or at least let them know that I'm here.

00:43:32.260 --> 00:43:36.480
So I went in again, and I read again while I was there.

00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:41.440
And then I found out, I come home, but I find I get it.

00:43:41.440 --> 00:43:44.100
So I go over thinking I'm doing the part of Ripley.

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:45.280
Nobody bothered to tell me

00:43:45.280 --> 00:43:47.290
that I was doing the part of Lambert,

00:43:47.290 --> 00:43:50.340
until they call to do wardrobe.

00:43:50.340 --> 00:43:52.080
And they said, "Well, you need to come in

00:43:52.080 --> 00:43:55.960
"and to get your gear and stuff for Lambert."

00:43:55.960 --> 00:43:59.600
And I said, "Oh, I'm not playing Lambert, playing Ripley."

00:44:00.440 --> 00:44:02.590
"Oh no, that part's been cast."

00:44:02.590 --> 00:44:03.890
So I said, "Well, wait a minute.

00:44:03.890 --> 00:44:06.810
I haven't even read this script from another perspective."

00:44:06.810 --> 00:44:09.630
So here I am in England, I call back my agent,

00:44:09.630 --> 00:44:10.870
I said, "Aren't I doing Ripley?"

00:44:10.870 --> 00:44:12.050
And he said, "Yes."

00:44:12.050 --> 00:44:15.000
And I said, "Well, they said that I'm doing Lambert."

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:17.460
So I said, "Well, I'm gonna have to re-read this script

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:19.020
and see."

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:21.480
So I re-read it and it bothered me a lot

00:44:21.480 --> 00:44:23.980
that she was so weepy all the time.

00:44:23.980 --> 00:44:26.350
I mean, every time she seemed...

00:44:26.350 --> 00:44:30.000
But they convinced me that I was the audience's

00:44:30.220 --> 00:44:33.220
I was reflection of what the audience is feeling.

00:44:33.220 --> 00:44:36.820
What you wanted to avoid all the way through

00:44:36.820 --> 00:44:43.820
was these people looking like just ordinary, bland, nice, middle-of-the-road, middle-class people.

00:44:43.820 --> 00:44:45.820
That would not have... they had to be vivid.

00:44:45.820 --> 00:44:51.770
You know, you were stuck with them in this capsule, or whatever you want to call it, for a long time.

00:44:51.770 --> 00:44:55.770
And they had to be vivid and very clearly defined.

00:44:55.770 --> 00:45:00.000
When I read Alien, initially, I wasn't drunk.

00:45:00.920 --> 00:45:07.270
to the project. Ridley wasn't attached, it was, my understanding was going to be a two

00:45:07.270 --> 00:45:10.190
or three million dollar budget on it, and I thought, well you're not going to make an

00:45:10.190 --> 00:45:16.410
effective film for that, and you know, it's a nice piece of material, has potential, but

00:45:16.410 --> 00:45:22.950
you're not giving it that potential, and that I thought was that, and about a month later

00:45:22.950 --> 00:45:30.000
I got a call from England, one of the producers at the time, and told me that Ridley was

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:36.030
going to be directing it and that there was a budget that would realize the potential.

00:45:36.030 --> 00:45:39.950
And then it became something else. It became working with Ridley Scott and learning about

00:45:39.950 --> 00:45:43.120
filmmaking.

00:45:43.120 --> 00:45:48.770
What Ridley's vision, as far as the actors were concerned, was he wanted it very much

00:45:48.770 --> 00:45:54.600
to be not of any time or any world.

00:45:54.600 --> 00:46:00.000
So it was important there was a mixture of nationalities. I cannot remember if you were

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.170
Yafit Koto's character was written as such,

00:46:03.170 --> 00:46:06.560
but he was obviously perfect for that part

00:46:06.560 --> 00:46:09.680
because he's so vivid, a wonderful actor.

00:46:09.680 --> 00:46:12.940
- When I first met Ridley, first meeting we had,

00:46:12.940 --> 00:46:15.020
I said, I don't like science fiction films

00:46:15.020 --> 00:46:17.200
or monster pictures.

00:46:17.200 --> 00:46:22.620
I opened with that, which was obviously a good opening.

00:46:22.620 --> 00:46:25.460
And he said, well, actually, I don't either,

00:46:25.460 --> 00:46:27.750
but I think I can make something of this one.

00:46:27.750 --> 00:46:29.630
- The casting of

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:36.500
John Hurt. I think it is known that there was another actor originally playing that part.

00:46:36.500 --> 00:46:40.610
John Finch, who became ill and we had to recast.

00:46:40.610 --> 00:46:44.990
Probably identified at that moment very strongly by playing Macbeth for Polanski.

00:46:44.990 --> 00:46:50.930
I'd been impressed by Polanski's work anyway, but particularly also by Finch.

00:46:50.930 --> 00:46:54.600
And so he said, "Yeah, sure, I'd love to do this." He came on board.

00:46:54.600 --> 00:47:00.000
First day, first shot, he starts to look as yellow as this pencil.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:05.510
i thought god what's wrong and we did a couple of takes and he seemed to absolute lackluster no

00:47:05.510 --> 00:47:11.360
energy he's sagging in his seat and cut and uh i got up went over to him and said what's up you

00:47:11.360 --> 00:47:16.910
all right he said no i'm terrible so i feel terrible he said how do i look i said terrible

00:47:16.910 --> 00:47:22.780
and we had to lift him out of his seat and he was carried away and it turns out that he was a

00:47:22.780 --> 00:47:30.000
diabetic i was had been asked if i'd like to do alien but i had already committed to the south african

00:47:31.280 --> 00:47:32.990
So that was dropped.

00:47:32.990 --> 00:47:37.780
And the actor that was going to play Cain later, for one reason or another, had to drop

00:47:37.780 --> 00:47:38.750
out.

00:47:38.750 --> 00:47:44.660
That night, we had a quick regroup in the office, and that night I drove to Hampstead

00:47:44.660 --> 00:47:48.480
Village and met with John Hurt.

00:47:48.480 --> 00:47:50.540
Then they heard that I was then free.

00:47:50.540 --> 00:47:56.870
So Ridley Scott came to me and we talked till gone midnight, say, on the Monday.

00:47:56.870 --> 00:48:00.000
Prepared him, of course, for what it was.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:02.000
And he said, "So when do I start?"

00:48:02.000 --> 00:48:03.240
And I said, "Tomorrow morning."

00:48:03.240 --> 00:48:07.260
And on the Tuesday morning, I was on the set at 7:30.

00:48:07.260 --> 00:48:10.080
So it was not a usual film for me.

00:48:10.080 --> 00:48:12.380
The script didn't arrive, and I didn't mull it over,

00:48:12.380 --> 00:48:13.980
and I didn't decide this, that, and the other.

00:48:13.980 --> 00:48:18.170
I had a long pitch from Ridley, and a very interesting one, too.

00:48:18.170 --> 00:48:21.380
And by the end of it, I said, "OK, Ridley,

00:48:21.380 --> 00:48:25.080
you'd better send the car around now to take me out to the studio."

00:48:25.080 --> 00:48:30.000
I think life and acting and everything is so full of those things.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:35.160
what if it hadn't happened. Nobody knows. I think John Hurt would probably still have exactly the

00:48:35.160 --> 00:48:42.010
same career because he's done so much varied and wonderful work. That really brings us to Ian Holm

00:48:42.010 --> 00:48:47.870
who I think is the most wonderful actor and hadn't done anything like that at all before

00:48:47.870 --> 00:48:55.890
and Ridley was really thrilled that he wanted to do it and in those days he'd done very little film.

00:48:55.890 --> 00:49:00.000
He'd done a bit of television, but he was really very much a theatre star.

00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:03.960
Casting was extensive, and even though it's only for seven people,

00:49:03.960 --> 00:49:06.980
at the end of the day I think it was a pretty definitive cast.

00:49:06.980 --> 00:49:10.290
Casting is enormously important to

00:49:10.290 --> 00:49:14.990
the whole making of the film, to the director's concept of the film,

00:49:14.990 --> 00:49:19.740
because it does so much work for the director, just innately

00:49:19.740 --> 00:49:24.460
in who you cast. It gives the director so much

00:49:24.460 --> 00:49:28.000
to work with, or not, if it's not properly cast.

00:49:28.000 --> 00:49:30.000
So it is an extraordinarily

00:49:30.250 --> 00:49:33.650
is sort of a subtle skill that's difficult to explain

00:49:33.650 --> 00:49:37.130
but people who know about filmmaking understand it

00:49:37.130 --> 00:49:40.920
and audiences who don't know about it appreciate it

00:49:40.920 --> 00:49:43.800
without always understanding what they're appreciating.

00:49:43.800 --> 00:50:00.000
There was a lot of money, there was a lot of

00:50:00.090 --> 00:50:06.860
construction this was not a little movie and for a period of time i i didn't have a weekend

00:50:06.860 --> 00:50:11.620
off for 11 weeks if it's for killing it that's the way we'll do it i'm for that nothing else

00:50:11.620 --> 00:50:20.570
it got tense inside those corridors he loves working with smoke tons of smoke this beeswax

00:50:23.280 --> 00:50:30.000
sometimes i know they used to complain about me not sitting there having a heart to heart i didn't

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:36.330
I wanted them to feel insecure, I wanted them to be afraid, and that's why I'd never show

00:50:36.330 --> 00:50:48.060
them the alien.

00:50:48.060 --> 00:50:54.710
The whole thing pivoted on believing a number of rather fantastic things, and it was very

00:50:54.710 --> 00:51:00.000
clear that if the monster looked silly or the chest bursting scene looked ridiculous

00:51:00.600 --> 00:51:03.460
You know, the whole thing was a lost cause.

00:51:03.460 --> 00:51:10.210
My personal predominant writing emotion throughout that whole production was deep worry.

00:51:10.210 --> 00:51:14.950
For once in my career, create a really good movie.

00:51:14.950 --> 00:51:23.140
The elements were there, and at the same time, there were elements there that could bring it all down and turn it into, you know, kitty litter.

00:51:42.060 --> 00:51:47.470
shot at Shepperton Studios. Then it was a kind of slightly run down just outside of London. It had

00:51:47.470 --> 00:51:53.070
been a famous studio. The Who were in a soundstage trying out a laser show next door to us.

00:51:56.050 --> 00:52:00.000
There was a special effects unit at Bray. Studios were

00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:04.190
way at the other end of London. I actually left

00:52:04.190 --> 00:52:09.180
right when they started shooting. I thought, you know, Gordon was going to stay there.

00:52:09.180 --> 00:52:13.270
Came back when there were some problems and

00:52:13.270 --> 00:52:17.060
spent most of the next year there until we were finished.

00:52:17.060 --> 00:52:22.290
I went on the set several times, yes. Quite remarkable, actually.

00:52:22.290 --> 00:52:26.640
I didn't expect it to be quite as fascinating as I found it to be.

00:52:26.640 --> 00:52:30.000
I remember the first day I went on the set, they took

00:52:30.060 --> 00:52:34.830
through the whole workshop and showed me what they were doing and i was pleasantly surprised

00:52:34.830 --> 00:52:42.140
i seem to remember the length of the production was i think it must have been about 14 weeks

00:52:42.140 --> 00:52:47.660
um i know we went over a couple it was a tough schedule very very tough

00:52:47.660 --> 00:52:53.960
it was tough because she really was as always pushes the fabric it was tense because the

00:52:53.960 --> 00:53:00.000
budget was very low and there were within the first four days of shooting there were nine

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:06.240
producers and studio executives on the set just counting the minutes on Ridley and complaining

00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:13.330
and why wasn't he shooting and he was shooting so fast they gave him no pre-light time so he was

00:53:13.330 --> 00:53:18.670
trying to find the look in the first two days and one of the producers I remember came to me on the

00:53:18.670 --> 00:53:23.420
fourth day and said why is he taking so long and I said to him I don't understand what you're saying

00:53:23.420 --> 00:53:30.000
he's doing 40 setups 50 a day sometimes the stuff looks amazing I don't know what you're all doing

00:53:30.670 --> 00:53:32.130
And it did get to Ridley.

00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:35.410
I remember one instant, I was right next to him.

00:53:35.410 --> 00:53:36.670
He went off like a rocket.

00:53:36.670 --> 00:53:39.510
It was like power coming out and he punched his fist

00:53:39.510 --> 00:53:40.990
right through the ceiling of the bridge

00:53:40.990 --> 00:53:44.710
in just sheer frustration because he did not understand

00:53:44.710 --> 00:53:50.570
why everyone was on his back.

00:53:50.570 --> 00:53:56.690
Well, of course, our first day of shooting.

00:53:56.690 --> 00:54:00.000
And all of the things start popping and there was smoke.

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:02.000
There was so much smoke, it was unbelievable.

00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:05.000
The canisters were going and the smokers being pumped in

00:54:05.000 --> 00:54:10.430
because we're supposed to be on fire as we're crash-landing into this strange planet.

00:54:10.430 --> 00:54:12.430
Surprised none of us are dead.

00:54:12.430 --> 00:54:15.430
I mean, they didn't have codes like here, they have to have a water base,

00:54:15.430 --> 00:54:17.430
which took a long time for them to get,

00:54:17.430 --> 00:54:20.320
but over there, it was like smelt pots.

00:54:20.320 --> 00:54:23.650
You were so grimy when you left at the end of the day.

00:54:23.650 --> 00:54:29.480
I mean, between being sprayed with glycerin and the smoke and the beeswax,

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:36.040
John Mallow was the one who designed the costumes, and these costumes were, well, they were huge, they were padded.

00:54:36.450 --> 00:54:43.400
We had big plates, they were like football padding, only they were sort of very, very heavy plastic,

00:54:43.400 --> 00:54:52.340
and then we had these big bubbles on the top of our heads, and they had CO2 with canisters in the back to set off that smoke as we went along.

00:54:53.140 --> 00:54:55.800
I'm standing in that spaceship with a clip on, fastening it on.

00:54:56.210 --> 00:54:58.550
Within 15 seconds, I'm getting really hot.

00:54:59.070 --> 00:55:00.000
I don't get claustrophobic.

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:05.490
but I'm getting really hot and you could easily get phobic, right?

00:55:05.490 --> 00:55:09.600
I said, "Jesus, this doesn't work. You've got to keep them cool inside here."

00:55:09.600 --> 00:55:16.130
We have to have some kind of help with, you know, oxygen, correct mix, right?

00:55:16.130 --> 00:55:19.930
After all, we are in the 20th century. Isn't that possible?

00:55:19.930 --> 00:55:22.690
You've got aqualungs and you've got breathing apparatus.

00:55:22.690 --> 00:55:26.050
And that explained to me why it was difficult.

00:55:26.050 --> 00:55:30.000
We had hockey gloves that we had to wear.

00:55:30.480 --> 00:55:40.690
moon boots and this desert it was the largest in Europe it was huge it was a

00:55:40.690 --> 00:55:45.410
desert they shipped in all of the sand it was absolutely unbelievable and here

00:55:45.410 --> 00:55:49.390
we are we're supposed to trudge across well it was a heat wave it was a hundred

00:55:49.390 --> 00:55:54.090
and fifteen degrees our suits have been lined with nylon which makes you sweat

00:55:54.090 --> 00:55:58.540
anyway we just wore sort of like little leotards underneath the whole thing and

00:55:58.540 --> 00:56:00.000
then we'd get into this suit

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:02.080
and then off we go.

00:56:02.580 --> 00:56:06.960
Well, they had forgotten to put in any sort of escape for our breath.

00:56:07.080 --> 00:56:09.280
Well, if you're in there for any length of period of time,

00:56:09.320 --> 00:56:11.500
you start breathing carbon dioxide.

00:56:12.060 --> 00:56:16.850
I do remember I almost passed out because a lot of that stuff was untried.

00:56:17.810 --> 00:56:21.500
They put it on you and you just tried to see if it would work for you

00:56:21.500 --> 00:56:23.430
and I really could not breathe.

00:56:23.830 --> 00:56:25.700
We were passing out like flies.

00:56:25.960 --> 00:56:27.050
I mean, it was unbelievable.

00:56:27.050 --> 00:56:29.490
Poor John, by the time he'd get to the other end,

00:56:29.790 --> 00:56:30.000
they had to breathe.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:36.320
to have a nurse there with an oxygen tank because he would sweat so profusely in there he couldn't

00:56:36.320 --> 00:56:44.500
even see it would just be one big massive bowl of sweat i can remember john being very very unhappy

00:56:44.500 --> 00:56:50.700
about i think he found it pretty claustrophobic um shut up in the old in the helmet so they'd have

00:56:50.700 --> 00:56:55.570
the nurse give him some oxygen and stuff like that and we all right off we go again

00:56:55.570 --> 00:57:00.000
they couldn't see a problem until it came to the distant shot where when

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:03.470
And to make the set even look bigger, they had kids.

00:57:03.470 --> 00:57:06.250
And two of the kids were Ridley's kids.

00:57:06.250 --> 00:57:09.730
And they both passed out in those little suits.

00:57:09.730 --> 00:57:12.830
So then, obviously, there must have been something

00:57:12.830 --> 00:57:14.750
that was going on, so they gave his air.

00:57:14.750 --> 00:57:16.220
That was nice of them.

00:57:16.220 --> 00:57:18.040
I mean, Ridley pushes everybody.

00:57:18.040 --> 00:57:22.100
He tends to concentrate on all aspects of the frame.

00:57:22.100 --> 00:57:23.640
In other words, he's painting his picture.

00:57:23.640 --> 00:57:26.050
And so sometimes actors who are not used to this

00:57:26.050 --> 00:57:28.130
are used to the director sort of focusing on them

00:57:28.130 --> 00:57:30.000
and them only, whereas Ridley is concerned

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:34.320
about you know the look about the dust about the about the lighting about the

00:57:34.320 --> 00:57:36.900
the way things are dressed and things like that I think they found sometimes

00:57:36.900 --> 00:57:41.280
that they were probably being a little bit neglected and I remember Tom was had

00:57:41.280 --> 00:57:46.840
some kind of angry words about having to hang around a long time on set

00:57:46.840 --> 00:57:55.690
studio of course resisted a lot of what he was doing resisted the time he was

00:57:55.690 --> 00:58:00.000
taking I resisted some of the time he was taking it we

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:02.500
We had a full cast waiting for three days in one case.

00:58:02.500 --> 00:58:04.720
Now, Tom, in your time, okay?

00:58:04.720 --> 00:58:06.520
I mean, wait 'til I move.

00:58:06.520 --> 00:58:07.660
Yes, I will.

00:58:07.660 --> 00:58:19.070
Ready?

00:58:19.070 --> 00:58:20.150
Ready?

00:58:20.150 --> 00:58:23.320
And we all were getting pretty disgruntled.

00:58:23.320 --> 00:58:24.740
But it was all worth it.

00:58:24.740 --> 00:58:26.690
We all knew we were making a classic.

00:58:26.690 --> 00:58:30.000
The first week, I had to hold

00:58:30.410 --> 00:58:37.540
I had to, I think, grab Jones, Jonesy, and I was wearing glycerin because it was supposed

00:58:37.540 --> 00:58:40.590
to be hot and we were supposed to be sweaty.

00:58:40.590 --> 00:58:48.300
And we did this shot, and my face started to get very red, and I felt this incredible

00:58:48.300 --> 00:58:49.460
burning.

00:58:49.460 --> 00:58:52.020
And I looked at the cat, and luckily they called lunch.

00:58:52.020 --> 00:58:53.020
I went upstairs.

00:58:53.020 --> 00:59:00.000
I was crying because I realized I was allergic to Jones, and I thought, "Gosh, it was probably

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:04.800
hard for Ridley to find four matching cats. They're going to get rid of me, because I'm

00:59:04.800 --> 00:59:10.010
allergic to the cat. I was sure that it had been harder to find the cats than me, and

00:59:10.010 --> 00:59:15.150
maybe it was as hard, I don't know. But luckily, after I washed my face, it was the glycerin

00:59:15.150 --> 00:59:19.430
in cat hair that I was allergic to. But I remember thinking, I'm so out of here, you

00:59:19.430 --> 00:59:25.520
know. Everything was so claustrophobic on that set. I mean, that is one of those sets

00:59:25.520 --> 00:59:29.960
where you walked in, and there's the engine room. Well, that's fine.

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:31.480
But connected to that is the corridor.

00:59:31.840 --> 00:59:33.260
Connected to that was the cafeteria.

00:59:33.540 --> 00:59:35.160
Connected to that was the hospital.

00:59:35.520 --> 00:59:37.600
It was all connected, the ship.

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:40.500
So once you were on, you were on.

00:59:40.680 --> 00:59:43.340
You had to go through everything in order to get off.

00:59:43.820 --> 00:59:45.300
So you could actually walk around the set

00:59:45.300 --> 00:59:47.900
and come out in different places and never leave it.

00:59:49.110 --> 00:59:52.470
And that's, in a way, how Ridley liked it and shot it.

00:59:52.720 --> 00:59:55.860
And you couldn't see out, so you didn't know where you were.

00:59:56.020 --> 00:59:58.230
And it was almost like shooting on location,

00:59:58.450 --> 01:00:00.000
and that really did contribute.

01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:03.110
to the claustrophobia and the realism,

01:00:03.110 --> 01:00:04.670
I know it really affected the actors

01:00:04.670 --> 01:00:06.870
'cause they, no matter where they looked,

01:00:06.870 --> 01:00:09.450
they saw Nostromo, you know, long halls and everything.

01:00:09.450 --> 01:00:14.050
They never, with practical lighting, you know, all inside.

01:00:14.050 --> 01:00:17.480
- Because I was mostly off, off-Broadway actor,

01:00:17.480 --> 01:00:22.130
to be in this set that were these beautiful,

01:00:22.130 --> 01:00:26.440
majestic sets where you could roam freely

01:00:26.440 --> 01:00:28.480
and see the whole world of the film.

01:00:33.360 --> 01:00:37.520
I think when you're dealing with confined space, it's one of the very much used aspects

01:00:37.520 --> 01:00:39.220
of the thriller as well.

01:00:39.220 --> 01:00:44.550
It's the very center of the pieces like We Clow and so on, you know.

01:00:44.550 --> 01:00:45.550
There is no escape.

01:00:45.550 --> 01:00:49.510
That is one of the bases of the real tension of the film.

01:00:49.510 --> 01:00:54.180
It's a low-key subject, they're very, very tight areas.

01:00:54.180 --> 01:01:00.000
Quite difficult to light in places, so unless you're going to swamp something with front

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:08.890
light then. It was a bit complex, especially as so much of the movie was shot hand-held

01:01:08.890 --> 01:01:15.350
with Ridley, although he did some form of blocking. It didn't mean to say it was particularly

01:01:15.350 --> 01:01:24.320
where he was going to end up. He also storyboards everything. He does small sketches. It's brilliant.

01:01:24.320 --> 01:01:30.000
You know, you get the light, the shade, the whole caboodle, all that. So it was his choice

01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:36.810
lenses. It was his choice of where he was going with that particular camera. I suppose

01:01:36.810 --> 01:01:43.990
my effort was more in towards, I covered most of it with a second camera. Tended to do the

01:01:43.990 --> 01:01:51.140
tighter shots when his camera wasn't in my shot. Ridley was really subjective. He was

01:01:51.140 --> 01:01:55.900
operating the camera, so I don't think he could really pull back. We sensed right at

01:01:55.900 --> 01:02:00.000
the top that he wasn't able to pull back and be objective and come to us and talk to us

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:08.350
So being with an extraordinary group of actors allowed him to do what he was doing in terms

01:02:08.350 --> 01:02:13.600
of just composing this magnificent looking film, looking through the camera.

01:02:13.600 --> 01:02:16.600
He did something no director has ever done before or since.

01:02:16.600 --> 01:02:20.550
He had about five or six pages of each character.

01:02:20.550 --> 01:02:27.480
And this is the actor's work usually, but he had my whole background.

01:02:27.480 --> 01:02:29.960
How many missions I'd flown.

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:35.360
My mother and father, my family had my whole history written out on paper.

01:02:35.900 --> 01:02:39.800
It was one of the things about Dallas' character, and for the other characters, too.

01:02:39.860 --> 01:02:42.020
There was no history. It was hard to really...

01:02:42.020 --> 01:02:43.960
It was about the alien, let's face it.

01:02:44.000 --> 01:02:47.730
It wasn't about these character buildings, any one of these individuals.

01:02:47.890 --> 01:02:49.970
That was something we had to realize as we went along.

01:02:50.470 --> 01:02:52.630
I think we ought to discuss the bonus situation.

01:02:52.730 --> 01:02:52.990
Right.

01:02:52.990 --> 01:02:56.050
Brett and I, we think we ought to... We deserve full shares, right, baby?

01:02:56.370 --> 01:03:00.000
Well, we were doing the breakfast scene, and it was very,

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:07.280
low energy I remember and I could tell that Ridley wasn't pleased but he was again behind the camera

01:03:07.280 --> 01:03:15.420
and as we walked away from the master with the group of all of us Ridley suddenly turned to me

01:03:15.420 --> 01:03:19.700
and he grabbed my hand he said look I don't want to give you your motivation to pick up your fucking

01:03:19.700 --> 01:03:30.000
teacup and I went and I burst into tears and I ran off and he caught up with me and he said oh I'm so sorry I'm

01:03:30.030 --> 01:03:32.790
"I'm really sorry." He said, "It's what I wanted to say to John,

01:03:32.790 --> 01:03:35.830
"but I couldn't say it to him, so I said it to you."

01:03:35.830 --> 01:03:38.990
It was his second film, and my first film,

01:03:38.990 --> 01:03:42.410
and he always said, you know, it was trial by fire for us.

01:03:42.410 --> 01:03:44.910
Over the years, I've learned to communicate differently

01:03:44.910 --> 01:03:48.300
with actors on different subjects.

01:03:48.300 --> 01:03:51.480
Sometimes it's best to say nothing

01:03:51.480 --> 01:03:55.310
and let them evolve, right, and then instruct.

01:03:55.310 --> 01:03:57.430
It was interesting. We'd find ourselves going off

01:03:57.430 --> 01:04:00.000
and having lunch together and going down to the pub and talking

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:05.010
And it actually became like these, you know, seven people on the ship.

01:04:05.130 --> 01:04:10.070
It was kind of interesting, and you sort of developed your own little character from that.

01:04:10.930 --> 01:04:17.290
Well, you know, I was from the theater, and I didn't realize that some actors really loved to improvise,

01:04:17.370 --> 01:04:18.890
and Yoffit was certainly one of those.

01:04:20.310 --> 01:04:24.710
And Ridley, at that point, he was operating,

01:04:24.710 --> 01:04:29.050
and I'm not sure how comfortable he felt with our little group.

01:04:29.620 --> 01:04:30.000
and

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:35.530
And I remember that one of the scenes where there was a lot of improvisation was a scene

01:04:35.530 --> 01:04:38.230
which he used to film the rehearsals.

01:04:38.230 --> 01:04:39.690
So we never had rehearsals.

01:04:39.690 --> 01:04:45.820
And everyone just sort of went for it, which was actually a great learning experience.

01:04:45.820 --> 01:04:52.560
And it helps the film because I think we were on edge and we were sort of into a survival

01:04:52.560 --> 01:04:53.570
mode.

01:04:53.570 --> 01:04:54.910
So I think it works very well.

01:04:54.910 --> 01:05:00.000
But at the time, it was hard for me because I came from such a different discipline where

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:04.040
where you are very faithful to the words of the writer.

01:05:04.040 --> 01:05:06.500
- Well, Jaffet's a big guy and quite formidable,

01:05:06.500 --> 01:05:11.140
and I think he tried to corner Ridley.

01:05:11.140 --> 01:05:12.860
There was a sort of a running joke

01:05:12.860 --> 01:05:16.400
where Ridley used to come up to us and say,

01:05:16.400 --> 01:05:18.780
which door is Jaffet standing by this morning?

01:05:18.780 --> 01:05:20.360
So that he could go into onto the set,

01:05:20.360 --> 01:05:22.060
fire another door where Jaffet wasn't there

01:05:22.060 --> 01:05:23.200
waiting to corner him.

01:05:23.200 --> 01:05:26.370
- Jaffet was always, always evolving.

01:05:26.370 --> 01:05:29.010
And in God bless him, driving me crazy,

01:05:29.010 --> 01:05:30.000
come to me with millions

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:35.650
ideas on a daily basis saying even to the extent of the day he would die saying you know I'm not

01:05:35.650 --> 01:05:40.240
going to die today I'm going to kill this son of a bitch you know and I think there was there

01:05:40.240 --> 01:05:45.930
was genuine tension between um funny enough the tension in the film between the the blow decks

01:05:45.930 --> 01:05:51.470
guys between yeah yeah fit and Harry Dean Stanton was sort of reflected to a degree actually in

01:05:51.470 --> 01:05:56.740
reality because I think Sigourney found it quite lonely being that the sort of you know the heroine

01:05:56.740 --> 01:06:00.000
amongst all these kind of tough guys and it's a certain certain

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:02.890
certain parts of that atmosphere, I think, carried on a bit.

01:06:02.890 --> 01:06:04.690
Would you get off my back, please?

01:06:04.690 --> 01:06:07.390
I'll be back as soon as we're on module 6.

01:06:12.390 --> 01:06:16.160
Sigourney had certain nervousnesses

01:06:16.160 --> 01:06:21.790
because Ridley wasn't inviting her at all to rushes,

01:06:21.790 --> 01:06:24.010
and it was an early movie for her,

01:06:24.010 --> 01:06:27.560
so she wasn't quite sure how she was coming over.

01:06:27.560 --> 01:06:30.000
Sigourney was the new kid on the block,

01:06:30.250 --> 01:06:33.400
And they all felt that she wasn't really

01:06:33.400 --> 01:06:35.830
doing that good a job in the scenes with her.

01:06:35.830 --> 01:06:38.670
