'cause we used the same landscape then.

01:22:21.780 --> 01:22:25.110
Later, I said, I wanna build the landing leg

01:22:25.110 --> 01:22:27.670
to show the scale of the Nostromo.

01:22:27.670 --> 01:22:30.000
So we built this 58 feet,

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:36.890
landing leg foot which still didn't look big enough and so I thought make up

01:22:36.890 --> 01:22:42.050
three tiny spacesuits and this will look twice as big and so they did and we put

01:22:42.050 --> 01:22:46.120
one of the cameraman's kids in one suit and Jake and Luke went into the other

01:22:46.120 --> 01:22:51.520
suits and because it was high I literally shackled them on so they're on

01:22:51.520 --> 01:22:56.090
so they couldn't move and if you over crank when they get off and walk of

01:22:56.090 --> 01:23:00.000
course they become adults. Suddenly the the spaceship leg

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:10.690
was like doubled in scale so it made it absolutely massive. There were the

01:23:10.690 --> 01:23:16.190
inevitable tensions between 20th Century Fox and and Ridley and everything like

01:23:16.190 --> 01:23:20.330
that because Ridley was pushing the envelope. I mean designing sets that had

01:23:20.330 --> 01:23:25.620
kind of one hadn't seen sets like that sort of at that time since Kubrick and

01:23:25.620 --> 01:23:30.000
2001. I mean absolutely incredible. A tribute to the

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:36.200
the Shepard Plaster Department because there were all those Giga sculptures kind of and sets brought to life were absolutely amazing.

01:23:37.000 --> 01:23:42.060
Gradually down the line I realized it made a lot of sense to have Giga design everything that was to do with the alien.

01:23:42.060 --> 01:23:50.550
That includes the landscape and the spacecraft and that everything that was earthly that it would be Michael Seymour and his team, right?

01:23:51.110 --> 01:23:55.130
And so that made a lot of sense, you know, two separate brains working on two different worlds.

01:23:56.060 --> 01:24:00.000
I did some drawings, so Ridley's got

01:24:01.210 --> 01:24:05.700
I like to have the exterior landscape,

01:24:05.700 --> 01:24:10.780
the space jockey, the ex-silo, exterior, interior,

01:24:12.390 --> 01:24:14.760
all the alien surroundings.

01:24:14.760 --> 01:24:19.530
And I was very happy to do that because I could,

01:24:19.530 --> 01:24:21.650
I was not so limited.

01:24:21.650 --> 01:24:25.790
They had not money to do it like I designed it.

01:24:25.790 --> 01:24:30.000
You know, the whole thing looks a little bit like Art Nouveau.

01:24:30.570 --> 01:24:34.050
It was not organic enough, organic-like.

01:24:34.680 --> 01:24:39.310
It looked like handmade from artists.

01:24:40.060 --> 01:24:42.850
The eggs, the eggs have been very good.

01:24:43.430 --> 01:24:44.720
They look real organic.

01:24:45.570 --> 01:24:48.350
Yeah, the eggs and the area where the eggs have been.

01:24:48.350 --> 01:24:52.530
And also the walls, they have been very good

01:24:52.530 --> 01:24:56.170
because this was planned to go in the egg silo.

01:24:57.000 --> 01:25:00.000
There was a round, like a...

01:25:01.190 --> 01:25:03.310
And that was very nice.

01:25:04.350 --> 01:25:07.530
I thought bones, yeah, they are dried and old,

01:25:07.710 --> 01:25:10.250
but they brought these fresh from the slaughterhouse,

01:25:10.610 --> 01:25:12.830
and there was a smell horrible

01:25:12.830 --> 01:25:16.880
because the inside of the bones had been...

01:25:17.370 --> 01:25:18.590
that was just fresh,

01:25:18.870 --> 01:25:22.550
and we had a big box full of bones,

01:25:22.550 --> 01:25:26.890
and there was such a smell that we...

01:25:26.890 --> 01:25:30.000
Even myself, I wouldn't see that.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:32.480
I said, "Please go away with this."

01:25:32.480 --> 01:25:35.970
I only used dried bones, old,

01:25:35.970 --> 01:25:38.790
together with plaster scene to do it.

01:25:38.790 --> 01:25:41.540
The scale was not wrong.

01:25:41.540 --> 01:25:42.960
It was too big.

01:25:42.960 --> 01:25:47.600
We couldn't find tubes and things of this size.

01:25:47.600 --> 01:25:50.420
So that didn't work very well.

01:25:50.420 --> 01:25:55.160
So Ridley always used smoke, everything, dark and smoke,

01:25:57.420 --> 01:26:00.000
and that makes the thing much better.

01:26:00.000 --> 01:26:01.580
than it is.

01:26:01.580 --> 01:26:03.220
I want to have a landscape

01:26:03.220 --> 01:26:05.960
who is very much organic-like,

01:26:05.960 --> 01:26:09.170
and I would like to have old motors

01:26:09.170 --> 01:26:12.570
from motorcycle or things like that,

01:26:12.570 --> 01:26:15.670
big things to make a wonderful landscape,

01:26:15.670 --> 01:26:19.240
but there was no time and no money for this.

01:26:19.240 --> 01:26:23.160
A real mixed up, a real biomechanical landscape,

01:26:23.160 --> 01:26:24.850
that would be good.

01:26:24.850 --> 01:26:27.780
- Giger's sets were so erotic,

01:26:27.780 --> 01:26:30.000
and I mean, his big vagina,

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:34.320
and penises and I mean it's like you know it's the whole thing is like you're

01:26:34.320 --> 01:26:40.900
going inside of some sort of womb or whatever I mean it's sort of visceral

01:26:40.900 --> 01:26:44.800
and all of those things and I think all those elements came into it and and

01:26:44.800 --> 01:26:51.230
that's what makes it really work on this so-called space jockey which Giger by

01:26:51.230 --> 01:26:56.290
the way designed that whole set the alien and he hand brushed the alien

01:26:56.290 --> 01:27:00.000
airbrushed it himself and got on a scaffold and airbrushed

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:04.520
the whole set so it would look have that eerie texture and but the thing was this

01:27:04.520 --> 01:27:07.720
they didn't want to give us it was too expensive a set didn't want to give it to

01:27:07.720 --> 01:27:11.380
us they said well look you're only in here one time it's different if you keep

01:27:11.380 --> 01:27:15.580
using this set this is massively expensive this whole big creature and

01:27:15.580 --> 01:27:19.240
this big back wall there's no we can't give you this it's absolutely too

01:27:19.240 --> 01:27:22.120
expensive I think it was like four or five hundred thousand dollars they said

01:27:22.120 --> 01:27:26.430
this is not your main set you're just gonna have to walk by and see a

01:27:26.430 --> 01:27:30.000
a skeletal imprint in the mud of this

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:34.090
15 foot creature and then you'll walk into this strange looking building, it

01:27:34.090 --> 01:27:37.870
doesn't have to have these immense walls, it'll be a bunker or something that's

01:27:37.870 --> 01:27:42.200
weird, it's constructed at angles and that's it we can do that cheaper and

01:27:42.200 --> 01:27:45.500
that's where we gotta stop you, some of these things you're just going wild.

01:27:45.500 --> 01:27:49.120
But the space jockey was one of those things I said look I can't cheat this you

01:27:49.120 --> 01:27:54.900
know it's got to be real. It's kind of the cockpit or the driving deck of

01:27:54.900 --> 01:28:00.000
whatever this craft was and and Giga had done this drawing which I love

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:02.170
And I said, this is going to be it.

01:28:02.170 --> 01:28:04.820
But Ridley and I kept after him.

01:28:04.820 --> 01:28:08.260
We said, look, we need this because this is our Cecil B. DeMille shot.

01:28:08.260 --> 01:28:10.590
You've got to have this so that when they see this,

01:28:10.590 --> 01:28:14.430
they know it's just not a little like we were going to do a Roger Korn movie.

01:28:14.430 --> 01:28:18.080
You've got to have the people walking in, not miniatures,

01:28:18.080 --> 01:28:20.390
camera pull back, and they're on this thing.

01:28:20.390 --> 01:28:23.810
And it's the most amazing set you've ever seen, your jaw drops.

01:28:23.810 --> 01:28:25.990
And you know you're watching an A-level movie.

01:28:25.990 --> 01:28:30.000
Ridley sort of was, you know, loves having sets, you know.

01:28:30.120 --> 01:28:37.720
We would go on discussing ideas for sets and things and so he would want something more.

01:28:37.720 --> 01:28:42.640
So I would get designed like storerooms and other pieces of action.

01:28:43.320 --> 01:28:58.960
And I kept building sets and the guy from Fox, who was kind of very concerned about the fact we were running over schedule for like four weeks, was doing his best to stop that happening.

01:28:59.180 --> 01:28:59.600
So...

01:29:00.160 --> 01:29:02.180
He would find out where I was burning his head

01:29:02.180 --> 01:29:05.230
and have them pulled down, so we never got to shoot them.

01:29:05.510 --> 01:29:06.790
That happened on two or three occasions.

01:29:07.300 --> 01:29:10.740
There's this set, which I think was an air shaft set or something,

01:29:11.640 --> 01:29:14.610
and there was this continual tussle with Ridley saying,

01:29:14.970 --> 01:29:16.830
I want that set, I want to shoot on that set.

01:29:17.350 --> 01:29:20.830
And Peter Beale would go around knowing exactly what Ridley was like,

01:29:20.930 --> 01:29:23.090
he'd discovered over the weeks of shooting,

01:29:23.530 --> 01:29:28.250
and he would actually do sort of like a march around the studio

01:29:28.250 --> 01:29:30.000
and looking at all the stages to make sure

01:29:30.090 --> 01:29:32.610
that all the sets that were being built there was nothing that was being built

01:29:32.610 --> 01:29:36.430
without his approval and I seem to remember it was this tussle of him

01:29:36.430 --> 01:29:39.750
saying that is not going to be built we're cancelling that set and Ridley's

01:29:39.750 --> 01:29:43.690
saying now build it build it I mean you know it's one of those things that we

01:29:43.690 --> 01:29:46.810
could have gone on and on and on you know we'd have built other things and

01:29:46.810 --> 01:29:50.860
bigger things and more spaceship and more hidden crannies I mean there's whole

01:29:50.860 --> 01:29:55.000
sequences which were cut out the sequence where initially the alien

01:29:55.000 --> 01:30:00.000
creature gets Scarret the the captain and the

01:30:00.000 --> 01:30:07.090
then he cocoons him and he's stuck to a wall in one of the storerooms and he's become sort

01:30:07.090 --> 01:30:15.380
of food for the alien creature and he's just glued to the wall. There's no way she can

01:30:15.380 --> 01:30:30.000
rescue him so she burns him. Walking on those sets, walking on those stages and seeing the

01:30:30.000 --> 01:30:35.360
richness of design and texture. It began to get to you, literally physically, and then

01:30:35.360 --> 01:30:39.970
of course when we started to shoot and Ridley pumped the smoke in there, you were now in

01:30:39.970 --> 01:30:45.930
ghostly surroundings. What one realized is that we were doing, that Ridley was bringing

01:30:45.930 --> 01:30:53.720
this picture to the highest possible creative level, design level, acting level, lighting

01:31:39.120 --> 01:31:44.170
He was very strange looking, dressed all in black, you know, with this Swiss accent.

01:31:44.170 --> 01:31:49.180
And when we were looking at his work before we met him, we weren't really sure we wanted to meet him.

01:31:49.180 --> 01:31:54.000
We were kind of disturbed about, is he really going to be here, you know?

01:31:54.000 --> 01:32:00.000
I like if they are disturbed, yes, that's good.

01:32:00.120 --> 01:32:08.560
I hate if they don't look at it or if I have people they like my stuff or others they hate it.

01:32:08.560 --> 01:32:16.980
He's a wonderful artist. I think that was a great move having him design the whole film.

01:32:16.980 --> 01:32:30.000
He was so strange. Most people kept him the creeps. They wanted his office way across the room than the other because

01:32:30.120 --> 01:32:33.740
He was like Dracula, Peter Lorre or something.

01:32:33.740 --> 01:32:36.780
He was always dressed in black and he had a very soft voice.

01:32:36.780 --> 01:32:38.460
But there was always these horrible stories.

01:32:38.460 --> 01:32:39.940
We heard that he had the skeleton

01:32:39.940 --> 01:32:42.080
from his fiance who had committed suicide

01:32:42.080 --> 01:32:43.880
and people gave him the creeps.

01:32:43.880 --> 01:32:44.860
I got along with him great.

01:32:44.860 --> 01:32:47.540
Dan and I and Ridley got along superbly.

01:32:47.540 --> 01:32:51.360
Everyone else sort of thought he was so weird

01:32:51.360 --> 01:32:53.220
that they didn't work with him too much.

01:32:53.220 --> 01:32:55.180
And as a result, that was great for Dan and I

01:32:55.180 --> 01:32:59.200
because we got so much input getting along with him so well.

01:32:59.200 --> 01:33:00.000
People would kind of

01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:04.980
shy away from him. They say he was obsessed in his work. He had bones of all kinds all

01:33:04.980 --> 01:33:05.980
over the place.

01:33:05.980 --> 01:33:15.370
The light hurts me. If you have too much sun you like to be in the shadow, and I like always

01:33:15.370 --> 01:33:19.060
to be in the shadow, in the dark.

01:33:19.060 --> 01:33:26.130
I liked him a lot. He had a girlfriend called Mia, and they both dressed in black. He was

01:33:26.130 --> 01:33:30.000
very pale skinned as well, and he was sort of...

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:34.310
He was sort of spooky in a way, but I also kind of liked him.

01:33:34.310 --> 01:33:35.590
And there was a sweet side to him.

01:33:35.590 --> 01:33:37.270
He brought his mother over, you know.

01:33:37.270 --> 01:33:40.710
His mother had hip replacements, and so he would take her out of him.

01:33:40.710 --> 01:33:45.060
I remember having dinner out one evening with him and his mother and Mia.

01:33:45.060 --> 01:33:49.570
The giga was... I mean, we spent a lot of time together.

01:33:49.570 --> 01:33:53.020
The eggs were designed and obviously made,

01:33:53.020 --> 01:33:56.510
and they would be the way up they were with the aperture at the top,

01:33:56.510 --> 01:34:00.000
with a hydraulic opening, which would open, looking very threatening.

01:34:00.760 --> 01:34:02.720
with Jimmy Shields' great sounds on it.

01:34:02.720 --> 01:34:06.720
It's still one of the great sounds when it opens

01:34:06.720 --> 01:34:10.960
and you hear the visceral, viscera, and the strength.

01:34:10.960 --> 01:34:13.040
You know it's strong.

01:34:13.040 --> 01:34:16.180
The detail that he looks at later

01:34:16.180 --> 01:34:19.810
would be shot at Bray when the film is finished.

01:34:19.810 --> 01:34:22.710
So we'd ship one of those eggs out to Bray

01:34:22.710 --> 01:34:26.080
and to make it really strange, because if you watch carefully,

01:34:26.080 --> 01:34:30.000
the fluid on it is going upwards, so then you feel

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:34.810
that over and that always freaked them out when they saw that dripping upwards and then

01:34:34.810 --> 01:34:41.930
inside it was semi-transparent like a large piece of jelly and uh all it was was a fiberglass

01:34:41.930 --> 01:34:49.580
with mostly glass than fiber in it so it's nearly transparent and um i needed it to move

01:34:49.580 --> 01:34:54.330
when we were in brisbane and bray i kept looking he's got he's dead so have sticks

01:34:54.330 --> 01:35:00.000
in poking look terrible so we worked out a method of as it was about to open

01:35:00.640 --> 01:35:03.680
It kind of went like that, lit a little bit.

01:35:03.680 --> 01:35:05.960
And then I think he looks at it again,

01:35:05.960 --> 01:35:09.900
and inside are my hands with two rubber gloves on,

01:35:09.900 --> 01:35:11.180
washing up gloves.

01:35:11.180 --> 01:35:16.060
And all I'm doing is going like that.

01:35:16.060 --> 01:35:18.790
And so that was how that was done.

01:35:31.790 --> 01:35:35.500
And of course, after that, he comes in and looks into the aperture, opens.

01:35:36.010 --> 01:35:36.890
He backs off.

01:35:37.850 --> 01:35:40.040
We say, don't go there.

01:35:40.370 --> 01:35:41.850
He goes there, looks in.

01:35:42.330 --> 01:35:47.090
And what he's staring at is what they call Nottingham Lace, which is fundamentally cow's stomach.

01:35:47.970 --> 01:35:52.160
That lacy, fleshy stuff is what we call tripe.

01:35:52.610 --> 01:35:55.250
And so I've got buckets of this in white plastic buckets.

01:35:55.250 --> 01:35:59.030
And I'd have to put on a white coat and gloves and handling this stuff.

01:35:59.150 --> 01:36:00.000
And I just dressed it.

01:36:00.000 --> 01:36:08.510
on it. And then I couldn't find a whiplash effect of anything that you made, like a hose

01:36:08.510 --> 01:36:15.110
that was delicate enough. So I'd seen this intestine, sheep's intestine, which is actually

01:36:15.110 --> 01:36:22.330
beautiful. It's gossamer. Gossamer. Got little lines in it. Well, it was here in fragments

01:36:22.330 --> 01:36:27.250
because all you do is cover the bits and then say, it's not enough to have him look

01:36:27.250 --> 01:36:30.000
and it explodes. You need every moment of it.

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:33.760
and slowed down. So we slowed it down because it was really fast.

01:36:34.100 --> 01:36:37.860
You turn an airline on, it just goes, whash! It happens in less than a second.

01:36:38.680 --> 01:36:42.300
Whereas I think we stretched that to maybe one and a half seconds, maybe.

01:36:42.950 --> 01:36:47.110
And then it hits him as he goes back. I've already got it attached to his face.

01:36:47.740 --> 01:36:50.880
So you need that bridge between him coming in to look,

01:36:52.820 --> 01:36:56.550
him looking in, what he sees, and this is like that.

01:36:56.970 --> 01:37:00.000
Explosion up into the camera and...

01:37:00.000 --> 01:37:03.000
Well, I've got this attached to his face as he falls backwards.

01:37:03.000 --> 01:37:12.880
The face-hugger, that was my first thing I did for Dan O'Banion.

01:37:12.880 --> 01:37:16.020
When he sent me, I think, about a thousand dollars

01:37:16.020 --> 01:37:19.900
to do some paintings for Alien,

01:37:19.900 --> 01:37:24.920
and he gave me explanation and drawings,

01:37:24.920 --> 01:37:28.690
and he said that he's jumping on a big egg.

01:37:28.690 --> 01:37:30.000
This egg is about...

01:37:30.780 --> 01:37:34.080
And I thought that must be quite a big monster,

01:37:34.260 --> 01:37:36.540
so I did an enormous facehugger.

01:37:38.260 --> 01:37:40.550
And he was jumping.

01:37:40.730 --> 01:37:47.890
I thought he could jump when his tail is curved like a spring devil or whatever,

01:37:48.550 --> 01:37:49.710
the check in the box.

01:37:50.720 --> 01:37:55.140
Later on, he said I should do it smaller.

01:37:55.140 --> 01:38:00.000
So I think something with human hands is always scary.

01:38:00.030 --> 01:38:00.750
very scary

01:38:00.750 --> 01:38:01.290
so I

01:38:01.290 --> 01:38:02.510
I had the

01:38:02.510 --> 01:38:02.850
finger

01:38:02.850 --> 01:38:03.630
the long

01:38:03.630 --> 01:38:05.190
fingers

01:38:05.190 --> 01:38:06.510
what is the

01:38:06.510 --> 01:38:07.550
most important

01:38:07.550 --> 01:38:08.570
part on the

01:38:08.570 --> 01:38:09.350
facehugger

01:38:09.350 --> 01:38:10.550
and then

01:38:10.550 --> 01:38:11.870
it's a part

01:38:11.870 --> 01:38:13.210
there's a

01:38:13.210 --> 01:38:14.590
little sexual

01:38:14.590 --> 01:38:15.440
like

01:38:15.440 --> 01:38:16.390
yeah

01:38:16.390 --> 01:38:16.850
and

01:38:16.850 --> 01:38:18.550
the tube

01:38:18.550 --> 01:38:19.450
for

01:38:19.450 --> 01:38:20.740
that

01:38:20.740 --> 01:38:21.840
this beast

01:38:21.840 --> 01:38:22.460
has to

01:38:22.460 --> 01:38:24.140
to put

01:38:24.140 --> 01:38:24.560
in the

01:38:24.560 --> 01:38:25.200
in the

01:38:25.200 --> 01:38:25.720
mouth

01:38:25.720 --> 01:38:26.900
and then

01:38:26.900 --> 01:38:28.020
I had these

01:38:28.020 --> 01:38:28.500
hands

01:38:28.500 --> 01:38:30.000
in front

01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:35.000
and then Dan O'Banion, I drove them sidewards.

01:38:35.280 --> 01:38:36.000
That was better.

01:38:38.720 --> 01:38:41.860
I thought, you can't beat this.

01:38:42.320 --> 01:38:43.560
The real thing's always better.

01:38:43.780 --> 01:38:47.990
So then I had orders coming from a very good fishmonger in Shepparton Village

01:38:47.990 --> 01:38:53.460
who had oysters, clams, and other parts of fish,

01:38:54.240 --> 01:38:57.930
a little bit of sturgeon, a little bit of caviar.

01:38:58.440 --> 01:38:59.960
I got them to make a cup.

01:39:00.540 --> 01:39:06.680
with a container in rubber, which is the face hugger with the tail, the stinger, the like

01:39:06.680 --> 01:39:12.660
ray, manta ray tail, and the hands. That was all articulated. Inside it, this was just

01:39:12.660 --> 01:39:19.240
receptacle, and I just put in, you know, with a pair of tweezers, put in four oysters, one,

01:39:19.240 --> 01:39:25.420
two, three, four, and put in a large kidney, and put in a so-and-so, so-and-so, and that

01:39:25.420 --> 01:39:29.480
way you go. You can't do better than that. So you then hunkered it with his...

01:39:30.380 --> 01:39:36.230
with his instrument and the legs move and it gives you a shock.

01:39:36.230 --> 01:39:43.040
The chestburster, that was a very, very heavy scene.

01:39:43.040 --> 01:39:46.460
I think the strongest thing in the whole film,

01:39:46.460 --> 01:39:52.050
the idea came from a painting from Bacon, Francis Bacon.

01:39:52.050 --> 01:40:00.000
