sinews into the rubber suit.

01:56:51.930 --> 01:56:57.860
So suddenly the rubber suit isn't like, it should have been as thin as a contraceptive

01:56:57.860 --> 01:57:00.000
and as strong as nylon, right?

01:57:00.190 --> 01:57:04.090
because then I can crush this person into the suit and have all kinds of things built

01:57:04.090 --> 01:57:08.230
and stitched into it. I need someone extraordinarily thin, because whatever you did, there was

01:57:08.230 --> 01:57:12.220
a stuntman who was pretty thin, we put him in and he didn't look right. Funnily enough,

01:57:12.220 --> 01:57:19.200
that week, the casting director had been standing in a pub and stared at this guy who looked

01:57:19.200 --> 01:57:28.020
like probably close to seven feet, and very slender, very thin, kind of elegant. His name

01:57:28.020 --> 01:57:30.000
was Balaji.

01:57:30.000 --> 01:57:33.240
guy had gone over to him in the pub and said, you know, you know, the old line, how would

01:57:33.240 --> 01:57:39.480
you like to be in movies? And the guy had a kind of North London accent. And he was

01:57:39.480 --> 01:57:45.590
a graphic designer. So he turned up with great, very bemused and curious. And he was then

01:57:45.590 --> 01:57:48.960
attached to the film for the next 10 months. And so he did all the stuff where he had to,

01:57:48.960 --> 01:57:55.640
like coming out the wall in the shuttle at the end, in the corridor coming around the

01:57:55.640 --> 01:58:00.000
corner when Sigourney's trying to get out with the cats. When he approaches the

01:58:00.000 --> 01:58:05.720
Veronica Cartwright with his tail, and we get this sense of something really hideous

01:58:05.720 --> 01:58:08.970
because you hear her off camera.

01:58:08.970 --> 01:58:14.540
They built the suit to him, which was just unbelievable, I mean.

01:58:14.540 --> 01:58:20.180
His hands were below his kneecaps when he would just stand normally, so they just literally

01:58:20.180 --> 01:58:25.550
put gloves on him with elongated hands, and they put that head on him.

01:58:25.550 --> 01:58:30.000
They sent him to Tai Chi and to my classes so that he

01:58:30.000 --> 01:58:38.200
could learn to slow down and manipulate you know roll up and he when he first

01:58:38.200 --> 01:58:46.130
gets me he's in a down position and he comes up and rolls out and it was just

01:58:46.130 --> 01:58:56.290
so creepy I always felt keep it really tight and only see the bits you want to

01:58:56.290 --> 01:58:59.730
see because this is always stronger than anything else

01:59:00.000 --> 01:59:01.860
So if you think you've seen something,

01:59:01.860 --> 01:59:04.850
it's always gonna be probably more horrific and horrifying.

01:59:04.850 --> 01:59:08.960
- I asked Ridley, "Why are you shooting very little of alien?"

01:59:08.960 --> 01:59:13.240
And Ridley said, "Carlo, the audience,

01:59:13.240 --> 01:59:16.140
"if terror, they don't see nothing.

01:59:16.140 --> 01:59:18.410
"They don't see the dark area,

01:59:18.410 --> 01:59:21.510
"because expecting the monster possible here,

01:59:21.510 --> 01:59:27.070
"possible here, it is maximum the level of the suspense."

01:59:30.120 --> 01:59:37.620
"No, no, it's good. I shoot this little, be quiet. Your movie, your monster, people show, but very little."

01:59:37.620 --> 01:59:43.430
Well, actually, we see quite a lot of the beast, and you certainly get a good old look at the chestburster.

01:59:43.430 --> 01:59:49.440
You get a terrific look at the air. You get a great look at the other elements and the evolutions,

01:59:49.440 --> 01:59:53.450
and I think you get enough plenty. You get a great look at the head, which is beautiful.

01:59:53.450 --> 01:59:55.650
Harry Dean's standing, the thing's hanging there.

01:59:55.650 --> 02:00:00.000
Now, that was a gymnast that they got and put in that suit, and he was up curling

02:00:00.000 --> 02:00:04.440
up in the chains. The amazing thing was when they shot up, you couldn't even

02:00:04.440 --> 02:00:10.010
tell that he was up there. The way he uncoiled and came down and then put his

02:00:10.010 --> 02:00:15.970
arms out, and I mean it did, it looked like, you know, that crucifix. Christ is

02:00:15.970 --> 02:00:21.070
ascending, I'm going to take your life now. It was like very bizarre and very

02:00:21.070 --> 02:00:27.590
sort of gothic, which goes a lot with what the whole Giger gothic thing is.

02:00:31.760 --> 02:00:34.360
The poor guy couldn't sit down, you know,

02:00:34.360 --> 02:00:37.080
so they made a special swing for him.

02:00:37.080 --> 02:00:39.880
Tom Skerritt said, "This guy can't walk around like this.

02:00:39.880 --> 02:00:42.400
"This is ridiculous, do something for this poor person."

02:00:42.400 --> 02:00:45.360
They hadn't even thought of how he was gonna sit down,

02:00:45.360 --> 02:00:47.720
'cause you have that tail and that rubber suit.

02:00:47.720 --> 02:00:50.580
I mean, that poor thing was in that suit all day long.

02:00:50.580 --> 02:00:54.050
- I came to work one day right at the lunch break,

02:00:54.050 --> 02:00:55.730
so the big doors were open,

02:00:55.730 --> 02:01:00.000
and out of the soundstage came the crew and the...

02:01:00.280 --> 02:01:02.200
alien monster still in his suit

02:01:02.200 --> 02:01:04.970
but with the head off, thin as a nail.

02:01:05.990 --> 02:01:08.940
And he's walking with the wardrobe lady

02:01:08.940 --> 02:01:13.760
who's five feet, and they're talking politics

02:01:13.760 --> 02:01:15.780
with a very intense conversation

02:01:15.780 --> 02:01:17.360
as they're walking off to lunch.

02:01:17.980 --> 02:01:20.760
And he's got these tennis shoes on

02:01:20.760 --> 02:01:23.220
that are bright blue, Adidas.

02:01:24.620 --> 02:01:30.000
And the tail is being carried by another wardrobe guy

02:01:30.000 --> 02:01:32.920
who was a rather flamboyant guy

02:01:32.920 --> 02:01:36.370
who had this white ascot blowing in the wind.

02:01:36.370 --> 02:01:38.400
It was one of those,

02:01:38.400 --> 02:01:41.070
I wish I could have photographed that one.

02:01:41.070 --> 02:01:43.870
- We used to have a dresser, his name was Mother.

02:01:43.870 --> 02:01:45.270
We called her Mother.

02:01:45.270 --> 02:01:47.930
And he was in charge of doing the alien.

02:01:47.930 --> 02:01:49.390
Of course, there was always supposed to be

02:01:49.390 --> 02:01:52.240
a lot of slime and stuff on him.

02:01:52.240 --> 02:01:54.980
- KY Jelly was very much in fashion at the time.

02:01:54.980 --> 02:01:58.830
And I think that was put on everything that moved

02:01:58.830 --> 02:02:00.000
in front of the camera.

02:02:00.000 --> 02:02:04.990
on the alien on was dripping from everywhere I have never seen bigger tubes

02:02:04.990 --> 02:02:10.590
of K.Y. jelly they were like gigantic sizes and then you could have mother

02:02:10.590 --> 02:02:15.630
going I'll do it and then he would come running out with his huge big tubes of

02:02:15.630 --> 02:02:21.740
K.Y. and start caressing this poor alien's ass rubbing it and making it

02:02:21.740 --> 02:02:28.320
glisten and oh god it was hysterical we did have some good times

02:03:07.640 --> 02:03:13.730
I was a sound editor for 17 years and the last film that I did as sound editor was The

02:03:13.730 --> 02:03:14.660
Duralists.

02:03:14.660 --> 02:03:19.670
So that's where I met Ridley and we got on extremely well, I thought.

02:03:19.670 --> 02:03:24.550
And I got a call from his office to find out if I would be free to do the sound on a new

02:03:24.550 --> 02:03:27.170
film he was going to do called Alien.

02:03:27.170 --> 02:03:29.510
So I phoned back and said thank you very much.

02:03:30.000 --> 02:03:33.470
but I'll cut it, but I'm not gonna do the sound.

02:03:33.470 --> 02:03:35.110
So he phoned up and he said,

02:03:35.110 --> 02:03:37.570
"Well, you better come and meet the producers of this film."

02:03:37.570 --> 02:03:41.060
So I did, and I met David Guyler and Gordon Carroll.

02:03:41.060 --> 02:03:43.260
And after spending half an hour with them

02:03:43.260 --> 02:03:44.930
and Ridley and stuff, I said,

02:03:44.930 --> 02:03:46.940
"Well, can I do the film or not?"

02:03:46.940 --> 02:03:48.940
They said, "Of course."

02:03:48.940 --> 02:03:51.280
Which I tell you was one of the great thrills of my life

02:03:51.280 --> 02:03:54.260
because it was gonna be a fantastic film.

02:03:54.260 --> 02:03:57.450
You knew reading the script, it was gonna be interesting.

02:03:57.450 --> 02:04:00.000
And to work with Ridley, who I admire from

02:04:00.410 --> 02:04:02.810
The Duelist, I didn't know him apart from that.

02:04:02.890 --> 02:04:06.100
I knew he did commercials and did the fantastic commercials.

02:04:06.850 --> 02:04:08.190
But Duelist was wonderful.

02:04:08.950 --> 02:04:11.790
And I thought, well, what he made that look like?

02:04:11.850 --> 02:04:14.250
What's he going to make a science fiction movie look like?

02:04:30.000 --> 02:04:36.700
Editing was in a trailer in EMI, and then finally at Bray.

02:04:36.700 --> 02:04:41.740
The editing process on this, plus mix, music and mix, because it's Jerry Goldsmith's score,

02:04:41.740 --> 02:04:44.920
I think probably was, maybe 20 weeks.

02:04:44.920 --> 02:04:52.510
Now I can post from 12 to 16 weeks, but I think it's partly, you know, the avid is faster.

02:04:52.510 --> 02:04:59.160
Don't forget, in those days you're using Hollywood Movieola and Kem, right, or Steenberg.

02:05:00.480 --> 02:05:02.540
I used to like that, I used to like hands-on

02:05:02.540 --> 02:05:09.030
and Terry Rawlins would go and have the films brought from the shelf

02:05:09.030 --> 02:05:12.670
and he'd run it on the Hollywood movie, choose a moment,

02:05:13.470 --> 02:05:15.930
then cut it in, put it on the steamboat, then check it,

02:05:16.050 --> 02:05:17.450
and then nah, do it again.

02:05:17.730 --> 02:05:19.930
Now it's like press button, bang, bang.

02:05:21.360 --> 02:05:23.350
When I read it, I thought to myself,

02:05:23.470 --> 02:05:26.570
this is going to be one of the scariest things if we can get it right.

02:05:27.490 --> 02:05:30.000
And I think the way we did get it right

02:05:30.250 --> 02:05:36.010
is by keeping it slow, funny enough, which is completely different than what they do

02:05:36.010 --> 02:05:45.620
today. And I think the slowness of it made the moments that you wanted people to be sort

02:05:45.620 --> 02:05:52.460
of scared, then we could go as fast as we like because you sucked people into a corner

02:05:52.460 --> 02:05:54.180
and then attacked them, so to speak.

02:05:54.180 --> 02:06:00.000
And I think that's how it worked. I mean, I like to go on the set as much as I can. In

02:06:00.000 --> 02:06:01.480
I could get away from the cutting room

02:06:01.480 --> 02:06:03.240
because I like to be there to hear

02:06:03.240 --> 02:06:05.400
what he's talking to the actors about

02:06:05.400 --> 02:06:07.970
and then I can talk to him myself about

02:06:07.970 --> 02:06:09.860
what does he want out of this scene

02:06:09.860 --> 02:06:12.040
and he would tell me what his plan is

02:06:12.040 --> 02:06:13.560
and why he's shooting it a certain way.

02:06:14.370 --> 02:06:15.830
I just couldn't get over the look of it.

02:06:17.090 --> 02:06:18.550
You know, the sets were fantastic,

02:06:18.770 --> 02:06:21.690
the lighting was great and performances.

02:06:22.710 --> 02:06:23.690
And it was just like,

02:06:23.830 --> 02:06:25.970
I think I said once before working with him,

02:06:26.050 --> 02:06:27.930
I did Legend and Blade Runner with him.

02:06:28.410 --> 02:06:30.000
And it was like...

02:06:30.640 --> 02:06:34.560
When you get his rushes it's like walking through an art gallery, everything is great,

02:06:34.560 --> 02:06:40.340
you want to dwell on everything. So it's deciding what are you going to use out of all this

02:06:40.340 --> 02:06:46.910
material because it looks fantastic. You know what you've got to achieve from it, but how

02:06:46.910 --> 02:06:52.870
you achieve it after all these years is very difficult to sort of pin down. I did that

02:06:52.870 --> 02:07:00.000
because of this or because of that. The facehugger, when it flies out of the egg, well I have

02:07:00.000 --> 02:07:06.670
various angles on on the stuff coming out including it and what you try to do

02:07:06.670 --> 02:07:12.320
is to get it like trying to get it to unwind come out like it's unwinding so

02:07:12.320 --> 02:07:15.680
you'd use a few frames of this and a couple of frames of that and few frames

02:07:15.680 --> 02:07:23.610
that so that you had this thing well when you look at the first cut up the

02:07:23.610 --> 02:07:27.990
film like alien you've really got to use your imagination because there's no

02:07:27.990 --> 02:07:30.000
sound for one thing lots of those scenes

02:07:30.000 --> 02:07:31.960
were shot with all these noisy machines

02:07:31.960 --> 02:07:34.620
or effects working on the stages.

02:07:35.710 --> 02:07:37.550
So you used to run all these things silent.

02:07:37.950 --> 02:07:40.230
You sit there and look at him creeping around

02:07:40.230 --> 02:07:43.320
in the rain room silent, and it's boring.

02:07:44.500 --> 02:07:46.320
So you've got to imagine what it's going to be like

02:07:46.320 --> 02:07:48.480
with all these jingling chains in the rain.

02:07:49.780 --> 02:07:51.000
So it was like all those things,

02:07:51.060 --> 02:07:53.060
creeping around corridors with nothing happening.

02:07:53.680 --> 02:07:55.400
But when you've got the music effects and everything.

02:07:56.000 --> 02:07:59.600
So that was one of the things you had to sort of combat

02:08:00.120 --> 02:08:09.440
Okay, put that on the screen for me. Can you get closer?

02:08:09.440 --> 02:08:12.360
It was 3 hours and 12 minutes, the first cut of it.

02:08:12.360 --> 02:08:17.920
And I knew it was going to be great, but I said, "Oh, no, I just hope that people don't get carried away with pretensions,"

02:08:17.920 --> 02:08:21.300
because I remember Ridley's words, "I want this to be unpretentious."

02:08:21.300 --> 02:08:26.450
So I had no input other than to beg them to say, "Please cut an hour out, no matter what you think.

02:08:26.450 --> 02:08:30.000
It has to be close to a two-hour movie that you think you're losing,"

02:08:30.350 --> 02:08:32.270
Everything's there. You don't have to cut out any big moments.

02:08:32.350 --> 02:08:33.830
It shouldn't move this slowly.

02:08:34.250 --> 02:08:36.370
And they said, don't worry. We know, we know, we know.

02:08:36.650 --> 02:08:37.190
We know we can't.

02:08:37.230 --> 02:08:39.490
Nobody wants to lose an extra screening, and we'll get it down.

02:08:39.550 --> 02:08:41.850
And the next time I saw it, it was a perfect length.

02:08:41.890 --> 02:08:42.670
It was a two-hour movie.

02:08:44.010 --> 02:08:47.080
Length was an issue, and we had obviously shot the sequence

02:08:47.080 --> 02:08:51.250
where Dallas is found in the ship as an egg still alive,

02:08:51.330 --> 02:08:52.410
and she torches him.

02:08:53.170 --> 02:08:54.770
And I know that...

02:08:54.770 --> 02:08:58.590
I seem to remember we had to take about 11 minutes out of the movie,

02:08:58.850 --> 02:08:59.730
and...

02:09:00.600 --> 02:09:06.660
Funny is something that, in fairness, that scene never, ever really worked quite as well as it should be

02:09:06.660 --> 02:09:09.480
because it just didn't really look realistic enough, him and the egg.

02:09:10.560 --> 02:09:15.960
I didn't think they were going to be able to use it because of the, when I read it in the script,

02:09:16.100 --> 02:09:20.040
it was sort of, it slowed the pace of the exit, of the end of the picture.

02:09:20.360 --> 02:09:23.680
The picture had to have that pace, her trying to get the hell out of there,

02:09:23.680 --> 02:09:29.890
and we're all rooting for her to get out of there, and for her to slow up and have a conversation with Dallas was...

02:09:31.020 --> 02:09:34.900
not appropriate. The last 17 and a half minutes of the film

02:09:34.900 --> 02:09:41.670
is Sigoni with no dialogue. And we are repping around those corridors.

02:09:41.670 --> 02:09:46.680
I remember when I first saw Alien, I was sitting in a projection room all by myself

02:09:46.680 --> 02:09:51.320
and of course everything was much over length. I remember to this day the one scene where

02:09:51.320 --> 02:09:54.550
he was looking for his cat and

02:09:54.550 --> 02:10:00.000
it was in this dark chamber and the ship and I remember this chain hanging down

02:10:00.120 --> 02:10:02.640
water dripping, and they hadn't cut it,

02:10:02.640 --> 02:10:06.630
and it went on and on, and I was absolutely terrified.

02:10:06.630 --> 02:10:08.370
You know, I kept saying, "It's just a movie.

02:10:08.370 --> 02:10:10.350
"It's just a movie," and it really scared

02:10:10.350 --> 02:10:12.010
the shit out of me, to be honest.

02:10:12.010 --> 02:10:14.540
I mean, I was terribly frightened with it.

02:10:14.540 --> 02:10:19.140
And, which is good, because that helps

02:10:19.140 --> 02:10:21.410
when I have to sit down and write the music for it.

02:10:21.410 --> 02:10:23.910
Because I like to go see a film, any film that I'm doing,

02:10:23.910 --> 02:10:25.750
I try to see it first as an audience

02:10:25.750 --> 02:10:29.220
and react as the audience, and try to distance myself.

02:10:31.240 --> 02:10:35.810
what I eventually have to do. Jerry Goldsmith's score is one of my favorite

02:10:35.810 --> 02:10:45.440
scores. Seriously threatening, but beautiful. I mean, it has beauty, darkness, and seems

02:10:47.140 --> 02:10:54.540
to play the DNA of some distant society, which you'd have to call it. You know, that space

02:10:54.540 --> 02:11:00.000
jockey would be from somewhere, and they would have some kind of order, social order or disorder.

02:11:00.030 --> 02:11:07.800
I always think of space as being the great unknown and not as terrifying but

02:11:07.800 --> 02:11:13.570
questioning and so there's an air of romance about it and that's why I like

02:11:13.570 --> 02:11:17.630
doing Star Trek because it's very romantic and it paints space that way

02:11:17.630 --> 02:11:21.860
so all the weird things and all that and I guess I approached Alien that way

02:11:21.860 --> 02:11:26.240
there was this air of mystery but there was sort of a beauty to it and the

02:11:26.240 --> 02:11:30.000
unknown and I thought well let me play the whole opening of

02:11:30.000 --> 02:11:34.940
romantically and very lyrically and then let the shock come as the this story

02:11:34.940 --> 02:11:41.830
evolves not in other words don't give it away in the main title so I wrote this

02:11:41.830 --> 02:11:46.980
very nice main title and it was there's sort of mystery but it was it was a

02:11:46.980 --> 02:12:00.000
lyrical mystery it didn't go over too well and really I

02:12:00.000 --> 02:12:04.570
had major disagreements over that so then I subsequently wrote a new main

02:12:04.570 --> 02:12:10.450
title which was the obvious thing weird and strange and which I really loved and

02:12:10.450 --> 02:12:14.760
I didn't love and it was very interesting consequently I kept getting kudos for

02:12:14.760 --> 02:12:18.160
years after on the main title I wrote for Alien which was not exactly my

02:12:18.160 --> 02:12:22.650
choice and the original one I wrote took me like a day to write it and the

02:12:22.650 --> 02:12:28.620
alternate one took about five minutes to write let me just say to start with I

02:12:28.620 --> 02:12:30.000
think the man is a

02:12:30.120 --> 02:12:37.160
genius I think he's done the greatest scores who I admire but I also feel he's

02:12:37.160 --> 02:12:45.050
very stubborn like I am myself I think and when it came to Alien I attempt the

02:12:45.050 --> 02:12:49.190
film myself with as much Jerry Goldsmith music as I could because I knew he was

02:12:49.190 --> 02:12:55.950
gonna do the film he's an absolute master of like doing a you know a rough

02:12:55.950 --> 02:13:00.000
temporary soundtrack etc you know just he's just got this great eye and

02:13:00.000 --> 02:13:02.820
ear and he says I know what piece of music you know would be great in there

02:13:02.820 --> 02:13:07.160
and so he always put the most in which is a real problem sometimes because you

02:13:07.160 --> 02:13:10.890
know you put you have this free range of you can choose any music you want and

02:13:10.890 --> 02:13:16.840
whenever I do these temps myself I obviously know by that time who's going

02:13:16.840 --> 02:13:20.470
to be the composer and I bend over backwards to find stuff that they've

02:13:20.470 --> 02:13:23.710
done in various other films to see if I can make it work obviously you can't

02:13:23.710 --> 02:13:30.000
always make it work so you have to use material from other sources but I just

02:13:30.000 --> 02:13:37.290
invaluable and i must say at times there's areas you can get to work better than they do later and

02:13:37.290 --> 02:13:44.810
that's not degrading anything composers do but i don't think anybody is closer to the film than

02:13:44.810 --> 02:13:50.830
the director and the editor will ever be i mean we live with this it's ours and the thing is when

02:13:50.830 --> 02:13:57.010
you're trying to create a temp score and if you're interested in music which i love it's a love of mine

02:13:57.010 --> 02:13:59.640
then uh

02:14:00.190 --> 02:14:02.110
You know the mood you want to create.

02:14:02.110 --> 02:14:05.420
You know what you want to try and say musically.

02:14:05.420 --> 02:14:08.170
And I don't think you should try and influence

02:14:08.170 --> 02:14:12.300
the composers too much, but if you get a mood correct,

02:14:12.300 --> 02:14:14.490
that's the mood I feel they should go with.

02:14:14.490 --> 02:14:16.730
They can change the theme, change the orchestration,

02:14:16.730 --> 02:14:19.780
but keep in mind the mood that we created.

02:14:19.780 --> 02:14:21.940
I remember when the pods opened, you know,

02:14:21.940 --> 02:14:25.710
from the hyper-sleep, they did eventually temp-track it.

02:14:25.710 --> 02:14:27.400
And in that particular scene,

02:14:27.400 --> 02:14:30.000
they had temp-tracked it with a piece of music I

02:14:30.160 --> 02:14:35.310
written for Freud and everyone thought that oh look how wonderful that music

02:14:35.310 --> 02:14:41.310
works I said it stinks it's all wrong I mean it it was a little lullaby that I

02:14:41.310 --> 02:14:48.300
had written for Freud so I wrote a piece I wrote a piece for that it was very

02:14:48.300 --> 02:14:54.630
impressionistic and very mysterious but but pretty but mysterious and and I

02:14:54.630 --> 02:14:56.120
thought it worked brilliantly

02:15:02.560 --> 02:15:08.440
it came out with the freud music in it consequently i got this more letters saying

02:15:08.440 --> 02:15:13.880
starting to repeat yourself eh they had bought the music from freud and put it in that spot so

02:15:14.680 --> 02:15:22.150
you could see that i was sort of like going at opposite ends of the of the pole with the filmmakers

02:15:22.150 --> 02:15:27.760
on the picture and i don't think he ever forgave me for for using like howard hanson for the end of

02:15:27.760 --> 02:15:30.000
the film and

02:15:30.250 --> 02:15:35.370
and keeping the music that he wrote for Freud for the film.

02:15:35.370 --> 02:15:39.530
Though that was Fox's decision, you know,

02:15:39.530 --> 02:15:41.530
they could have said we're using the music that's written,

02:15:41.530 --> 02:15:45.360
but I do think that what we did on our attempt

02:15:45.360 --> 02:15:47.950
in those areas was better than what he did.

02:15:47.950 --> 02:15:50.050
And I'm taking nothing away from the rest of the film

02:15:50.050 --> 02:15:53.530
because what he did was perfect for it.

02:15:53.530 --> 02:15:55.270
- Well, you know, this is always a tricky part

02:15:55.270 --> 02:16:00.000
because, you know, finally, no matter how much of it they'll

02:16:00.060 --> 02:16:05.390
the composer hums for you or plays on the piano or does anything like that, you know,

02:16:05.390 --> 02:16:08.790
you don't really hear his score until you're on this, you know, recording stage.

02:16:08.790 --> 02:16:11.890
At least that was the way he used to.

02:16:11.890 --> 02:16:17.030
And his score, when he, when it finally was way too lush and way too, more like his Patton

02:16:17.030 --> 02:16:23.060
score, you know, and which was not really what anybody particularly wanted.

02:16:23.060 --> 02:16:30.000
He wanted it more haunting and weird and strange and, and it wasn't really.

02:16:31.500 --> 02:16:37.500
So, actually what happened was we bought a couple of the Goldsmith's pieces that we had in the temp.

02:16:38.750 --> 02:16:45.940
And then in the dub, in the mix, you know, we really played down a lot of his stuff.

02:16:46.140 --> 02:16:50.670
We played up some kind of weird sounds that we had and all the rest of that stuff.

02:16:50.670 --> 02:16:54.150
This is really Ridley. When I say us here, I really mean Ridley.

02:16:54.970 --> 02:16:58.930
And, you know, and he did an amazing job in the dub.

02:16:59.290 --> 02:16:59.730
But...

02:17:00.000 --> 02:17:04.560
Mr. Goldsmith was quite unhappy when he saw the movie and, in fact, went to the studio.

02:17:04.680 --> 02:17:08.660
He'd done a lot of movies for Fox and, you know, announced that we were going to be a

02:17:08.660 --> 02:17:11.660
disaster and it was horrible and we'd ruined the score and we'd done all the rest of that

02:17:11.660 --> 02:17:11.920
stuff.

02:17:12.380 --> 02:17:19.370
They released it as an album and I bought the album and there were a couple of tracks

02:17:19.370 --> 02:17:24.470
on the album that weren't in the movie and they were tracks in which Goldsmith used horns

02:17:24.470 --> 02:17:30.000
to personify the alien and it was very effective.

02:17:30.670 --> 02:17:39.050
this creepy, distant honking that comes in that is clearly meant to be the alien's theme.

02:17:39.050 --> 02:17:48.360
And it's very effective, it's not in the movie, but then I can't really see any place in the

02:17:48.360 --> 02:17:51.510
movie that you could use it without it being intrusive.

02:17:51.510 --> 02:18:00.000
Ridley is a brilliant filmmaker, he's a brilliant visualist, and I think that was just his second

02:18:00.000 --> 02:18:04.450
and film, and he wasn't as articulate then about what music should do.

02:18:04.450 --> 02:18:14.140
I think he thinks so much in visuals, and music cannot replicate the visuals, it's not

02:18:14.140 --> 02:18:19.350
what it's supposed to do, and I think he was thinking more in terms, at least the message

02:18:19.350 --> 02:18:23.150
I was getting for him, he wanted me to be visual with the music.

02:18:23.150 --> 02:18:27.470
I can't be visual with the music, that's not what I'm supposed to do, let the director

02:18:27.470 --> 02:18:30.000
and the cinematographer put the visuals on the screen.

02:18:30.160 --> 02:18:32.410
let me do the emotional element.

02:18:32.410 --> 02:18:35.910
And I think that's where it became a big problem with me.

02:18:35.910 --> 02:18:37.610
And fortunately, I had a lot of time to do it.

02:18:37.610 --> 02:18:39.410
And I went back and redid music.

02:18:39.410 --> 02:18:44.680
And somehow it all worked out finally in the end.

02:18:44.680 --> 02:18:46.320
But it was a problem.

02:18:46.320 --> 02:18:49.060
And I think the biggest problem was just in communication

02:18:49.060 --> 02:18:51.770
between me and the director.

02:18:51.770 --> 02:18:55.080
It would be interesting if that film had been made today.

02:18:55.080 --> 02:18:58.900
And Ridley had now the benefit of having

02:18:58.900 --> 02:19:00.000
made so many other pictures.

02:19:00.000 --> 02:19:06.090
and worked with a lot of different composers, how he would have approached it with me musically.

02:19:06.090 --> 02:19:13.000
The real challenge of composing music for films is to write a score that works brilliantly in the film

02:19:13.000 --> 02:19:16.880
and also has a life of its own that stands by itself.

02:19:16.880 --> 02:19:23.110
I think many of the great scores of the past worked brilliantly in a film but taken by themselves.

02:19:23.110 --> 02:19:24.390
They don't stand alone.

02:19:41.960 --> 02:19:46.960
I had a call from Peter Beale, who was then head of FOX in the UK and Europe, I think,

02:19:46.960 --> 02:19:53.340
and he said he'd like me to meet Walter Hill. And I met Walter Hill, David Guiler,

02:19:53.340 --> 02:20:00.000
Gordon Carroll, up at FOX in London, in Soho. And they said, "Would I like to meet you?"

02:20:00.000 --> 02:20:04.910
to do Alien. And I'd had a script. I thought the script was outstanding because as you

02:20:04.910 --> 02:20:08.070
read it, you know, you knew it was going to be a good movie or I thought it was going

02:20:08.070 --> 02:20:16.890
to be a good movie. But I'd already signed to do Empire Strikes Back. And so I said,

02:20:16.890 --> 02:20:21.300
well, I'd love to do it, but it seems to clash with Empire. And Peter Beale said, well,

02:20:21.300 --> 02:20:26.640
it's another Fox production. I'll organize that. So he talked to Lucas and Gary Kurtz,

02:20:26.640 --> 02:20:30.000
I think, and I got the okay.

02:20:30.000 --> 02:20:36.410
The original concepts of Alien were basically a series of storyboards, very detailed storyboards.

02:20:36.530 --> 02:20:39.350
Ridley Scott is well known for storyboarding every single scene.

02:20:39.870 --> 02:20:41.930
We had the special effects storyboards.

02:20:42.330 --> 02:20:47.980
We also had a whole bunch of paintings done by Ron Cobb, who was the conceptual artist they used for many of the spacecraft.

02:20:48.720 --> 02:20:51.300
The strange thing was that we never actually followed them,

02:20:51.300 --> 02:20:56.320
because we kept on getting messages which would come from Shepparton, where the main unit was shooting,

02:20:56.640 --> 02:21:00.000
to Braze Studios where we were filming the models about 30

02:21:00.000 --> 02:21:03.920
The drawings and plans that came over seemed to be sort of a conglomeration,

02:21:03.920 --> 02:21:07.920
as if somebody had taken Ron Cobb's design and they'd altered it.

02:21:07.920 --> 02:21:13.920
And we obviously found out afterwards that Ridley Scott was having a sort of a say in this and changing things.

02:21:13.920 --> 02:21:19.840
And actually Nostromo, the main craft in the film, it kind of evolved,

02:21:19.840 --> 02:21:24.840
and it evolved many times over a period of months and months, over a year in fact it evolved.

02:21:24.840 --> 02:21:29.800
It originally started out as this basic space tug.

02:21:30.090 --> 02:21:34.920
We shot on this thing with it actually painted yellow for six months.

02:21:35.220 --> 02:21:37.700
And this was the time when Brian Johnson was involved in the film.

02:21:37.820 --> 02:21:40.740
However, Brian went off to work on Empire Strikes Back.

02:21:40.900 --> 02:21:44.750
And we painted the spacecraft grey on Ridley Scott's orders.

02:21:44.950 --> 02:21:48.600
And then when Ridley Scott finished shooting main unit stuff at Shepparton,

02:21:48.740 --> 02:21:50.100
came over to Bray Studios.

02:21:50.420 --> 02:21:52.520
And we basically started from scratch.

02:21:53.250 --> 02:21:58.560
Well, the plan was, the great plan, which of course Ridley had no intention of ever adhering to,

02:21:58.560 --> 02:22:00.000
was that the effects

02:22:00.000 --> 02:22:04.240
would be shot parallel with the main unit shooting,

02:22:04.240 --> 02:22:08.060
and Brian Johnson had that rather difficult task

02:22:08.060 --> 02:22:12.000
of actually doing that, and he shot some very nice stuff,

02:22:12.000 --> 02:22:14.910
but I know that Ridley knew that he could,

02:22:14.910 --> 02:22:17.550
in the same way that on, to jump to another movie,

02:22:17.550 --> 02:22:20.150
Blade Runner, he already wanted to look through the camera

02:22:20.150 --> 02:22:22.670
and line those shots up himself, which he did in the end,

02:22:22.670 --> 02:22:26.240
and it resulted in those incredible sort of city shots.

02:22:26.240 --> 02:22:29.220
Ridley was determined that he wanted to do them himself,

02:22:29.220 --> 02:22:30.000
So I think he could.

02:22:30.000 --> 02:22:36.000
Kind of consciously or unconsciously, he kind of said no, that's not quite right, that's

02:22:36.000 --> 02:22:40.340
a little bit too, I think he, in fairness, I think the shots were a little bit too colourful

02:22:40.340 --> 02:22:46.050
sometimes, and Ridley, I know you could grade them down, but Rid sort of had these specific

02:22:46.050 --> 02:22:47.050
ideas really.

02:22:47.050 --> 02:22:52.610
I was asked to organise a cameraman to work with the visual effects group.

02:22:52.610 --> 02:22:55.970
That I did, somebody I'd known several years before.

02:22:55.970 --> 02:23:00.000
I think Ridley kind of had words with the DP, I certainly think.

02:23:00.480 --> 02:23:04.300
with a DP, Denny Ayling.

02:23:04.300 --> 02:23:09.220
On the second Saturday of principal photography,

02:23:09.220 --> 02:23:14.160
Ridley told me he was going over to Bushy on the Saturday

02:23:14.160 --> 02:23:17.870
and asked that I go along there with him.

02:23:17.870 --> 02:23:22.910
And we went over there and we saw what Denny was doing

02:23:22.910 --> 02:23:27.640
for lighting and I went through a kind of lighting pattern.

02:23:27.640 --> 02:23:30.000
I felt a bit sorry for Denny

02:23:30.000 --> 02:23:37.080
he was trying to achieve something, where he couldn't achieve the stop that he wanted

02:23:37.080 --> 02:23:43.550
based on the lights that he'd got. I think he'd been terrorized by the production office

02:23:43.550 --> 02:23:50.190
about how much money he'd got to spend on lights, and he really didn't have enough.

02:23:50.190 --> 02:23:55.650
The risk of getting my own backside kicked, I ordered in a lot of other equipment.

02:23:55.650 --> 02:24:00.000
First of all, you get your model right. I mean, you get the model gnarly in age where

02:24:00.000 --> 02:24:03.770
and clear. You get the shape right and then you got to walk around it with the

02:24:03.770 --> 02:24:09.200
lights and this camera on that was Denny Ayling on all the effects and we'd walk

02:24:09.200 --> 02:24:12.020
around and I'd literally have him just on with the lights pulling the lights

02:24:12.020 --> 02:24:16.670
around and then just stand there and say stop and then come in and look at him

02:24:16.670 --> 02:24:22.420
Denny's saying okay it's too much here it is too much we've got to fill. So I

02:24:22.420 --> 02:24:26.410
always hated filling because I wanted to be really harsh but Denny would always

02:24:26.410 --> 02:24:29.320
force me into filling so we got to do a cover shot.

02:24:30.000 --> 02:24:36.140
Initially Ridley sort of viewed him with total and utter suspicion, but because he was actually a really good craftsman

02:24:36.140 --> 02:24:40.840
He actually knew his job inside out. I think actually really came to be rather fond of him

02:24:41.280 --> 02:24:46.350
What was cutting-edge which was actually we did not use was motion control and

02:24:47.230 --> 02:24:53.150
We didn't use it simply because the budget didn't allow us all that time to do stop-motion photography

02:24:53.390 --> 02:25:00.000
So we developed a camera which moved very slowly on a drive mechanism, but we didn't actually

02:25:00.000 --> 02:25:06.490
repeat moves as such because at the time the shots that were written in the

02:25:06.490 --> 02:25:12.560
actual script made it possible for us to do just regular model work with a high

02:25:12.560 --> 02:25:16.900
degree of professionalism and extremely wide-angle lenses. Some of these shots

02:25:16.900 --> 02:25:21.540
like the planet shots that was that very painful process where by the camera moves

02:25:21.540 --> 02:25:30.000
one tiny frame per second or something like that and so I remember really wasn't happy

02:25:30.000 --> 02:25:32.720
with what we were getting from it.

02:25:32.720 --> 02:25:34.800
So Ridley went in and shot it himself.

02:25:34.800 --> 02:25:36.400
And he said, "Put some smoke in there."

02:25:36.400 --> 02:25:37.440
And they said, "We can't do that.

02:25:37.440 --> 02:25:39.020
"The camera moves too slowly.

02:25:39.020 --> 02:25:40.520
"The smoke is gonna look, you know.

02:25:40.520 --> 02:25:42.470
"Put it in, you know?"

02:25:42.470 --> 02:25:45.510
And he did it and it worked, it looked great.

02:25:45.510 --> 02:25:48.930
- Because it wasn't on motion control pads,

02:25:48.930 --> 02:25:53.000
it was just operated, it was just physically operated.

02:25:53.000 --> 02:25:57.630
And because of that, I mostly kept the Nostromo close.

02:25:57.630 --> 02:26:00.000
So it didn't have to have stars,

02:26:00.090 --> 02:26:05.210
universe so that's all just live shooting with a lot of wind machine lost smoke

02:26:05.210 --> 02:26:08.150
of course people said there isn't there is no atmosphere in space I said there

02:26:08.150 --> 02:26:11.930
is now me to make it work I had no other way of doing I wouldn't have any sense

02:26:11.930 --> 02:26:16.570
of movement because I couldn't afford big galaxy shots of it working with

02:26:16.570 --> 02:26:22.650
Ridley Scott was was quite an experience it was nice to work directly with the

02:26:22.650 --> 02:26:26.250
director with nobody in between you know having a one-to-one relationship where

02:26:26.250 --> 02:26:30.000
I would go into a room do a little drawing and show it to him like

02:26:30.000 --> 02:26:31.200
of the clam palm.

02:26:31.870 --> 02:26:34.240
It was nice that he would actually listen to me.

02:26:34.240 --> 02:26:37.910
I mean, I wasn't anybody, really, I suppose, you know,

02:26:37.950 --> 02:26:42.150
and it was just great to have him actually consider your opinion as well,

02:26:42.310 --> 02:26:45.570
but then come along and sort of say, right, but I want it done this way.

02:26:45.770 --> 02:26:47.230
When you've got somebody like Ridley,

02:26:47.310 --> 02:26:52.820
who's very precise about what he wants in a general term,

02:26:53.050 --> 02:26:56.400
except he keeps changing his mind and building on things,

02:26:56.520 --> 02:27:00.000
he doesn't go from one thing to another, he just explains.

02:27:00.000 --> 02:27:08.430
expands and expands and expands. And so like on Nostromo, that started off as a small sort

02:27:08.430 --> 02:27:14.040
of model and then it grew bits, pieces everywhere. Ron Cobb had done the original design and

02:27:14.040 --> 02:27:17.640
then Ridley added bits to it. He kept saying, "Oh, let's put a bit on here and put a bit

02:27:17.640 --> 02:27:21.000
on there," because we were trying to build a large one, a large scale one, which was

02:27:21.000 --> 02:27:26.490
about 10 or 12 feet. And it kept growing and growing and growing. And I think it wound

02:27:26.490 --> 02:27:30.000
up being about 16, 17 feet in the end.

02:27:30.060 --> 02:27:36.490
challenge has got to be the fact that Ridley Scott wanted things changed, but he would want to shoot

02:27:36.490 --> 02:27:44.270
on a model very, very short time after he decided to make radical changes. An example of this would

02:27:44.270 --> 02:27:51.810
be that we had a very, very cathedral-like refinery, which I'd done a large amount of the

02:27:51.810 --> 02:27:59.010
work on. He was very much into the sort of spire look, this church spire type, gothic look,

02:28:00.000 --> 02:28:04.380
What he then did was he decided that he wanted a much chunkier-looking refinery,

02:28:04.580 --> 02:28:09.110
so he would basically attack this thing with a hammer and chisel.

02:28:09.270 --> 02:28:13.470
And if you imagine, you spent, say, six, eight weeks working on a section,

02:28:13.650 --> 02:28:17.550
and suddenly they would literally pick up a mallet and the one it's just like this,

02:28:17.710 --> 02:28:20.650
and you'd see bits flying off all over the place.

02:28:20.740 --> 02:28:24.520
And so the challenge was to...

02:28:24.990 --> 02:28:28.170
He'd then turn around and say, "Right, after lunch, I want to shoot on it,

02:28:28.350 --> 02:28:30.000
"and I want that bit like this and that bit like this."

02:28:30.000 --> 02:28:34.300
bit like that and you're thinking we just spent six weeks doing that bit and

02:28:34.300 --> 02:28:38.620
you want the shoot on this after lunch so one of the biggest challenges was

02:28:38.620 --> 02:28:44.320
getting the model looking good quick enough for him to shoot on it this is a

02:28:44.320 --> 02:28:48.340
sketch that I did at the time which was really just a doodle of the refinery

02:28:48.340 --> 02:28:54.780
showing the the spire like design it was just really for personal use to get a

02:28:54.780 --> 02:29:00.000
kind of a feel of how it would look as an overall design with the nostril

02:29:00.000 --> 02:29:04.000
The Nostromo right down here at the front, the domes underneath and then these massive

02:29:04.000 --> 02:29:06.990
spired pieces going up.

02:29:06.990 --> 02:29:11.450
These are the pieces which Ridley Scott later knocked off with his hammer and chisel.

02:29:11.450 --> 02:29:17.790
The Nostromo model weighed, I heard somebody say a quarter of a tonne, I don't know about

02:29:17.790 --> 02:29:20.050
a quarter of a tonne, but it was incredibly heavy.

02:29:20.050 --> 02:29:24.320
It needed to be lifted on a forklift truck, so the model had a huge metal framework built

02:29:24.320 --> 02:29:30.000
inside it and the Nostromo was plugged into by a forklift truck with a

02:29:30.000 --> 02:29:33.380
a couple of prongs on it, which actually fitted into the side of the model.

02:29:34.020 --> 02:29:38.620
And the way they achieved the Nostromo being pulled away from the refinery

02:29:38.620 --> 02:29:42.700
was that the arm itself wasn't actually motorised at all.

02:29:42.760 --> 02:29:45.100
It ran freely, and it was about 30 feet long.

02:29:45.380 --> 02:29:49.360
It was a really massive thing, again, clad with thousands and thousands,

02:29:49.360 --> 02:29:53.660
in this case, of Hornby Railway piers, all these criss-cross girder pieces,

02:29:53.940 --> 02:29:57.000
went into model shops and bought 150 of these kits.

02:29:57.620 --> 02:29:59.470
And when we told the guy what we were doing with them,

02:29:59.510 --> 02:30:00.000
I don't think that.

02:30:00.000 --> 02:30:00.680
believed us.

02:30:02.500 --> 02:30:04.160
And this shot

02:30:04.160 --> 02:30:05.920
was achieved by the

02:30:05.920 --> 02:30:08.520
forklift truck being covered in black velvet

02:30:08.520 --> 02:30:10.030
plugged into Nostromo

02:30:10.030 --> 02:30:12.510
and just driving away from the refinery

02:30:12.510 --> 02:30:14.470
dragging the Nostromo with it

02:30:14.470 --> 02:30:16.170
and of course as it pulled the

02:30:16.170 --> 02:30:18.250
Nostromo away, it dragged the arm

02:30:18.250 --> 02:30:20.310
with it. So that shot where you sit going

02:30:20.310 --> 02:30:22.390
out and coming away, it's just the forklift truck

02:30:22.390 --> 02:30:24.510
driving away on nice smooth

02:30:24.510 --> 02:30:25.630
on a nice smooth surface.

02:30:26.720 --> 02:30:28.510
Originally, the Nostromo

02:30:28.510 --> 02:30:30.000
had three huge

02:30:30.120 --> 02:30:35.100
arms which opened but Ridley Scott came up with this idea in an actual conversation where

02:30:35.100 --> 02:30:40.880
it would be like a great if a giant hand pushed the Nostromo away. So I did a very very quick

02:30:40.880 --> 02:30:45.720
sketch of this and showed it to him and we needed to detail it really quickly and we

02:30:45.720 --> 02:30:50.720
found that the legs of R2-D2 just happened to have this really nice shape and you can

02:30:50.720 --> 02:30:54.660
actually see them quite clearly clad on this thing.

02:30:54.660 --> 02:30:59.350
I think we had two or three different scales. We had one that was about four feet.

02:31:00.000 --> 02:31:04.520
and we had another one which was 12, 14, 15 feet long.

02:31:04.520 --> 02:31:06.190
And that one was very heavy,

02:31:06.190 --> 02:31:11.190
and it was cantilevered out like a forklift truck device

02:31:11.190 --> 02:31:14.190
so that on a beam, which was covered in black velvet,

02:31:14.190 --> 02:31:17.480
and because of all the dust and stuff that was around,

02:31:17.480 --> 02:31:20.500
you couldn't see any of the mechanism

02:31:20.500 --> 02:31:23.760
because we kept it lined up so the model was in front of it

02:31:23.760 --> 02:31:25.800
and the camera couldn't see it.

02:31:25.800 --> 02:31:27.080
And that was pretty difficult.

02:31:27.080 --> 02:31:29.920
I mean, that took a lot of effort.

02:31:30.090 --> 02:31:34.110
Lots of wind machines going, and plenty of vermiculite,

02:31:34.110 --> 02:31:37.130
which is an insulation for roofs and things,

02:31:37.130 --> 02:31:38.330
being blown around everywhere.

02:31:38.330 --> 02:31:42.100
Everybody had masks and goggles and everything else on,

02:31:42.100 --> 02:31:44.720
and earplugs on the lot.

02:31:44.720 --> 02:31:47.120
And in those days, we didn't have radio communications

02:31:47.120 --> 02:31:49.640
the way you have now, where everybody's got their own little

02:31:49.640 --> 02:31:50.800
set, and they can all join in.

02:31:50.800 --> 02:31:53.680
I mean, there was lots of shouting and screaming going on,

02:31:53.680 --> 02:31:55.020
thumping people on the back.

02:31:55.020 --> 02:31:56.170
"Stop."

02:31:56.170 --> 02:31:58.260
- Nick, could we do that in a--

02:31:58.260 --> 02:32:00.000
I mean, if we start level--

02:32:00.000 --> 02:32:03.000
and they'd put the light, you know, stick some lights on, et cetera, and reshoot it,

02:32:03.000 --> 02:32:05.000
and then he kind of created it in front of the camera,

02:32:05.000 --> 02:32:07.000
and it, although there wasn't always huge logic to it,

02:32:07.000 --> 02:32:10.820
I just think the actual landing of the ship had, you know, huge drama.

02:32:10.820 --> 02:32:16.170
It was very exciting, and I think, you know, Jerry Goldsmith's music and everything

02:32:16.170 --> 02:32:18.280
worked great at that particular point.

02:32:33.000 --> 02:32:37.180
He couldn't be burned off anything he had a passion about and so we all got fired with

02:32:37.180 --> 02:32:44.220
the same enthusiasm. So it was a labour of love. We did as much as we could to increase

02:32:44.220 --> 02:32:50.200
the production values on each of the shots and Ridley was the key to us doing that. He

02:32:50.200 --> 02:33:00.000
pushed and pushed and pushed us all the time. This is the sketch that I did of the

02:33:00.000 --> 02:33:05.980
Narcissus shuttlecraft, the original back section here having been built as a set and

02:33:05.980 --> 02:33:10.720
having been done as a drawing by Ron Cobb, the conceptual artist. I then came up with

02:33:10.720 --> 02:33:16.310
this idea of what the nose would look like and it was from this that the final model

02:33:16.310 --> 02:33:30.000
was constructed. And also just a very, very quick sketch showing the rear section. This was to enable

02:33:30.000 --> 02:33:38.520
me to work out the release clamp which I also built which drops the Narcissus down from the

02:33:38.520 --> 02:33:45.640
Nostromo and then releases. As it did so the word purge came up. When I went to see Blade Runner

02:33:45.640 --> 02:33:51.560
there's a shot where the car takes off and you see a screen and there's the clamp on from Alien on

02:33:51.560 --> 02:33:57.620
there which I'd actually designed and purge come up and I thought that is amazing I wonder how many

02:33:57.620 --> 02:34:00.000
people noticed that. We had an

02:34:00.000 --> 02:34:07.000
shot which required the burial shroud, where John Hurt's character Kane has died, to be

02:34:07.000 --> 02:34:13.700
fired out of the Nostromo's hatch. Now, many people would think there would be computerised

02:34:13.700 --> 02:34:19.850
ways of doing this, but not at all. I mentioned Nick Alder, who became the special effects

02:34:19.850 --> 02:34:26.680
director. We had a very, very large section of Nostromo built, about 40 feet long, which

02:34:26.680 --> 02:34:30.000
was supposed to be kind of the underside, like a trench.

02:34:30.000 --> 02:34:35.950
on the underside of Nostroma very reminiscent of the trench in Star Wars

02:34:35.950 --> 02:34:41.310
I'm sure there was a crossover there and what we actually did was I carved and

02:34:41.310 --> 02:34:45.450
again a given very short period of time to carve Kane's burial shroud in wood

02:34:45.450 --> 02:34:50.970
and I made a burial shroud about eight inches long and Nick Alder got hold of

02:34:50.970 --> 02:34:55.900
the fisherman's catapult for firing bait out into the pond where you're

02:34:55.900 --> 02:34:57.400
fishing and he just

02:34:57.400 --> 02:35:00.000
put a flat right down at the end of the studio

02:35:00.000 --> 02:35:04.240
And he just pulled the catapult back and fired my little model and it went

02:35:05.200 --> 02:35:09.550
Down the trench and because they filmed it at high speed so they came out in slow motion

02:35:10.090 --> 02:35:11.740
And he just did this over and over again

02:35:11.740 --> 02:35:16.060
And what they did was because it would drop eventually as it flew away eventually its trajectory like an arrow

02:35:16.060 --> 02:35:20.530
It would drop down, but they cut it before he did that and I just thought that was just the most amazing

02:35:20.770 --> 02:35:26.900
It's a simple way to get around a shot and just seeing him firing this thing of the fisherman's catapult

02:35:26.900 --> 02:35:30.000
He then started firing it at people with that's another story

02:35:31.050 --> 02:35:35.530
Later, he needed a shot where you saw Ripley, right at the end of the film...

02:35:35.690 --> 02:35:37.650
...inside the shuttlecraft, moving around.

02:35:37.810 --> 02:35:41.370
So we then had to make a foreshortened version of the shuttlecraft...

02:35:41.530 --> 02:35:43.570
...that had a TV monitor inside it.

02:35:43.730 --> 02:35:47.530
And that was a pre-recorded film of a girl...

02:35:47.600 --> 02:35:51.240
...not actually Sigourney Weaver, but her stand-in, running around inside.

02:35:51.400 --> 02:35:53.590
And that was built into the model of that.

02:35:55.660 --> 02:35:59.530
I think the only dodgy thing was always the marriage between...

02:36:00.280 --> 02:36:05.020
painting and model and the screen, it's always tricky, very tricky.

02:36:05.020 --> 02:36:10.410
But you know, even today in CGI, you can still see the whole flipping map.

02:36:10.410 --> 02:36:14.630
And I always like to go for wherever you can build a model,

02:36:14.630 --> 02:36:20.670
then photograph it with the light that's on the shot, then graft it onto it.

02:36:20.670 --> 02:36:26.630
CGI has probably, in my personal opinion, as a model maker,

02:36:26.630 --> 02:36:30.000
it's decimated much of the model making.

02:36:30.860 --> 02:36:37.600
I don't think, myself, that CGI will ever completely replace models.

02:36:37.600 --> 02:36:41.750
I don't care what anybody says, I think you can tell.

02:36:41.750 --> 02:36:46.770
There was a great deal of fun going on, some of the people involved in the film.

02:36:46.770 --> 02:36:51.250
We had Nick Alder, who was basically the special effects director after Brian left.

02:36:51.250 --> 02:36:53.150
He's a real prankster.

02:36:53.150 --> 02:37:00.000
I worked with him on Space 1999 and know what he was like, so the relationship was actually

02:37:00.000 --> 02:37:05.000
very good I got on very well with the guys I'd worked with them before with

02:37:05.000 --> 02:37:08.720
the actual crew itself it was a very happy relationship that we had bearing

02:37:08.720 --> 02:37:13.080
in mind we were working together for like I've said about 14 months technology

02:37:13.080 --> 02:37:16.360
advances you can think of new ways of doing things there were lots of things

02:37:16.360 --> 02:37:20.340
we could probably enhance the production value on we could have done huge moves

02:37:20.340 --> 02:37:24.760
through the vehicles and things which we didn't do but in actual fact because the

02:37:24.760 --> 02:37:30.000
script was so beautifully written and so tight and because Ridley was really clever with the way

02:37:30.000 --> 02:37:35.090
we didn't see alien but we sort of saw the alien and everything else we were just there to sort of

02:37:35.090 --> 02:37:39.410
remind people that there was a bit of space outside and they were billions of miles from anywhere

02:37:39.410 --> 02:37:44.900
we didn't need to do any more than that you know the production values enhanced what was going on

02:37:44.900 --> 02:37:52.490
in terms of the drama but um it didn't have to be a visual effects extravaganza as such to save a

02:37:52.490 --> 02:37:57.920
movie i mean we just enhanced what was already there as opposed to other movies some of recently

02:37:57.920 --> 02:37:59.280
that have come out which are

02:38:00.000 --> 02:38:04.640
effects, tour de forces, but no story. I mean, the key to this was the amazing story.

02:38:31.370 --> 02:38:39.190
The glory of the cinema is that it is an audience that is seeing it and therefore it is a collective understanding which is quite different from watching something on tape.

02:38:39.190 --> 02:38:51.910
It has a blend of the actual theatre itself even though what you're going to see is always exactly the same.

02:38:51.910 --> 02:38:58.450
It can vary from audience to audience according to the whatever collection of people you have there.

02:39:19.000 --> 02:39:25.140
first screening of the movie, which ran, I think, about four hours long from a 78-page

02:39:25.140 --> 02:39:30.000
screenplay, and we were supposed to go out to lunch afterwards.

02:39:30.000 --> 02:39:36.080
I was with my ex-wife and she was so traumatized, she couldn't leave the room for a day and

02:39:36.080 --> 02:39:37.100
a half.

02:39:37.100 --> 02:39:46.930
It was more violent and more frightening actually at the long cut that it ended up to be.

02:39:46.930 --> 02:39:52.640
We never really previewed it until we finished the film, which was interesting.

02:39:52.640 --> 02:40:00.000
But we finished it and we had a 70mm print and we took it to Fox and they insisted the

02:40:00.000 --> 02:40:03.560
arrived we just arrived that afternoon that they wanted to run it that night in

02:40:03.560 --> 02:40:10.860
their dubbing theatre the Zanuck Theatre and they invited all the science

02:40:10.860 --> 02:40:15.890
fiction sort of people who would come in dressed up as most of the creatures

02:40:15.890 --> 02:40:21.620
you'd ever seen to see the film and it ran terribly because the sound was so

02:40:21.620 --> 02:40:25.770
bad in there and we never could understand it because we we had a great

02:40:25.770 --> 02:40:30.000
soundtrack we've never changed it we had one in St. Louis where the sound wasn't quite right

02:40:30.220 --> 02:40:35.530
and it's just, it was, that was okay.

02:40:35.710 --> 02:40:37.590
- Ridley went crazy because he went down,

02:40:37.590 --> 02:40:39.330
checked the theater before the screening

02:40:39.330 --> 02:40:40.810
and two of the speakers were out

02:40:40.810 --> 02:40:42.820
and then he didn't want to screen the picture.

02:40:42.820 --> 02:40:45.970
- And by this time they are completely convinced

02:40:45.970 --> 02:40:49.470
that we'd made a bad soundtrack in England

02:40:49.470 --> 02:40:51.570
and the wrong thing had arrived.

02:40:51.570 --> 02:40:53.870
- But we were all quite worried after the one in St. Louis.

02:40:53.870 --> 02:40:57.880
And then in Dallas the next night,

02:40:59.090 --> 02:41:00.000
we had the sound,

02:41:00.000 --> 02:41:01.000
Absolutely right in the theatre.

02:41:01.540 --> 02:41:02.540
Let's get out of here.

02:41:03.600 --> 02:41:04.640
We've got this, though.

02:41:04.660 --> 02:41:05.540
It must go on.

02:41:06.050 --> 02:41:07.370
We arrive in this theatre,

02:41:07.960 --> 02:41:09.420
which was run by these guys.

02:41:09.520 --> 02:41:10.920
You could have eaten off their floor.

02:41:11.060 --> 02:41:12.760
The theatre was fantastic.

02:41:13.340 --> 02:41:14.460
The sound was great.

02:41:15.250 --> 02:41:17.170
It was the most incredible preview

02:41:17.170 --> 02:41:18.330
I've ever been in.

02:41:19.100 --> 02:41:20.380
I mean, people were screaming

02:41:20.380 --> 02:41:21.480
and running out of theatre.

02:41:21.760 --> 02:41:22.620
From what I remember,

02:41:22.740 --> 02:41:25.240
Ridley and Ivor Powell

02:41:25.240 --> 02:41:27.080
were holding down Alan Ladd Jr.,

02:41:27.080 --> 02:41:28.540
who was trying to get out of the cinema.

02:41:29.140 --> 02:41:30.000
The Usher...

02:41:30.800 --> 02:41:34.660
keeled over and not with a chestburster scene, strangely enough.

02:41:34.660 --> 02:41:39.550
People were just too stunned at that particular moment that they didn't react.

02:41:39.550 --> 02:41:41.630
This was a through-the-roof preview.

02:41:41.630 --> 02:41:43.630
This is when we knew we really, really had something.

02:41:43.630 --> 02:41:47.080
In fact, one of the ushers fainted.

02:41:47.080 --> 02:41:53.400
They were worried about violence in the theaters and all the rest of this kind of stuff.

02:41:53.400 --> 02:41:56.410
They said, "Oh, my God, there's an incident here. What's happened?"

02:41:56.410 --> 02:42:00.000
The usher had fainted when the robot's head came off.

02:42:01.760 --> 02:42:05.030
We had the management saying, "You know, this is terrible.

02:42:05.030 --> 02:42:09.250
"The people are being sick everywhere and it's in the state."

02:42:09.250 --> 02:42:10.810
We thought, "This is great."

02:42:12.870 --> 02:42:15.760
At first, I was standing in the back of the theatre and I was saying,

02:42:15.760 --> 02:42:19.600
"What's going on? People are getting a lot of walkouts."

02:42:19.600 --> 02:42:22.100
But what it was was that people were sitting close to the screen

02:42:22.100 --> 02:42:25.830
or walking back to stand and watch it in the back of the theatre.

02:42:25.830 --> 02:42:29.630
They didn't want to be that close to the screen, you know, because it was scary.

02:42:33.230 --> 02:42:37.020
some of the most exciting I think moments of my life because there's no greater buzz

02:42:37.020 --> 02:42:42.980
than being involved in a movie and suddenly not only was I going to the States for the

02:42:42.980 --> 02:42:46.920
first time in my life but I was actually being flown around with Ridley in the 20th Century

02:42:46.920 --> 02:42:49.460
Fox executive jet from preview to preview.

02:42:49.460 --> 02:42:56.430
We never had a premiere for this movie and I paid for my own ticket down at Egyptian

02:42:56.430 --> 02:43:00.000
Theatre to go and I stood in line to go see this movie.

02:43:00.440 --> 02:43:02.440
I mean, it was like so totally bizarre.

02:43:02.440 --> 02:43:05.290
I wanted to see it with an audience.

02:43:05.290 --> 02:43:07.750
People were getting up and leaving the theater

02:43:07.750 --> 02:43:11.170
and going out and barfing in the lobby.

02:43:11.170 --> 02:43:13.430
I mean, I guess it was just such a shock.

02:43:13.430 --> 02:43:15.630
I mean, we knew how it was made.

02:43:15.630 --> 02:43:19.170
It was, you know, our friend on the little dolly

02:43:19.170 --> 02:43:21.970
being squidgyed underneath the chair and stuff.

02:43:21.970 --> 02:43:24.170
But boy, I tell you, when it was cut together,

02:43:24.170 --> 02:43:27.990
it really was pretty amazing.

02:43:27.990 --> 02:43:29.540
- When we opened the film,

02:43:30.000 --> 02:43:34.520
I was staying at a friend's house in the Hollywood Hills there looking down and I could see the

02:43:34.520 --> 02:43:42.670
Egyptian theater where I was and they opened it and they ran it for 48 hours non-stop. It was just

02:43:42.670 --> 02:43:48.500
running like a loop for 48 hours. There was never a moment in that 48 hours that the queue wasn't

02:43:48.500 --> 02:44:00.000
around the block. I got up that day. I was a complete nervous wreck. I was angry. I was angry at the

02:44:00.000 --> 02:44:06.670
proper respect on this movie I wasn't even going to go to the opening the hell

02:44:06.670 --> 02:44:11.730
with them I'm not going to go to the opening of this thing Dan somehow had had

02:44:11.730 --> 02:44:15.370
a bad experience I remember this sticks out of my mind because of Dark Star he

02:44:15.370 --> 02:44:19.150
remembers coming and thinking oh my god they've been and nobody laughed and

02:44:19.150 --> 02:44:22.150
nobody responded and nobody came and when they did they didn't laugh they

02:44:22.150 --> 02:44:25.490
into jokes that's what he said to me I'll never try another comedy again so he

02:44:25.490 --> 02:44:29.920
kept on feeling this fear that it was gonna fall on its face like Dark Star did

02:44:30.160 --> 02:44:32.680
I jumped in my car and I just drove around streets.

02:44:32.680 --> 02:44:35.600
I drove around LA, crazily, nowhere.

02:44:35.600 --> 02:44:36.600
I just drove.

02:44:36.600 --> 02:44:37.450
"Hey, wait!"

02:44:37.450 --> 02:44:39.850
I was mad.

02:44:39.850 --> 02:44:42.010
"I'll show him, I'll this, I'll that."

02:44:42.010 --> 02:44:45.550
I guess he got drunk or something because he almost didn't want to come.

02:44:45.550 --> 02:44:46.550
He said, "I can't take it."

02:44:46.550 --> 02:44:50.080
"I'm not going to that."

02:44:50.080 --> 02:44:55.860
At 7:55 I suddenly turned the wheel and ran, I drove like a rocket into Hollywood.

02:44:55.860 --> 02:44:58.200
I drove up and I parked somewhere and I got out.

02:45:00.090 --> 02:45:05.350
And so there was a huge line of people that went down the block and around the block.

02:45:05.350 --> 02:45:07.080
And I said, "Is that for Alien?"

02:45:07.080 --> 02:45:11.680
And I got out and I walked the whole length of the line.

02:45:11.680 --> 02:45:18.290
I walked all this long line. They were all lined up to see Alien, my movie.

02:45:18.290 --> 02:45:28.160
And I sort of walked up, I drifted up towards the opening, the entrance there of the Egyptian.

02:45:28.160 --> 02:45:29.250
And I got up there.

02:45:30.190 --> 02:45:33.470
And there was Ronnie Chassette and a couple of people.

02:45:33.470 --> 02:45:36.930
He was like a man possessed.

02:45:36.930 --> 02:45:38.790
I mean, he was just like terrified.

02:45:38.790 --> 02:45:40.470
I said, "Where have you been? Come in here."

02:45:40.470 --> 02:45:42.210
They took me in and we went inside.

02:45:42.210 --> 02:45:44.370
I floated in at the theater.

02:45:44.370 --> 02:45:47.100
Every seat was full and they put me down

02:45:47.100 --> 02:45:51.590
in a seat somewhere and I sat there.

02:45:51.590 --> 02:45:56.990
I was kind of numb, like a dream.

02:45:56.990 --> 02:45:59.380
The house lights went down.

02:46:04.440 --> 02:46:12.300
The 20th Century Fox Lager came up, and the whole audience cheered.

02:46:14.120 --> 02:46:18.330
And we heard these tremendous shrieks and cheers and applause.

02:46:18.330 --> 02:46:23.070
And I brought the third scare in, and Dan was sitting behind me, and he started weeping.

02:46:23.070 --> 02:46:29.410
And my eyes flooded with tears, and the tears just ran down my face.

02:46:30.510 --> 02:46:34.200
and I just sat there and watched the movie.

02:46:34.200 --> 02:46:38.570
And I said, "Dan, Dan," and he said, "I can't believe it.

02:46:38.570 --> 02:46:40.510
It's real, it's not real."

02:46:40.510 --> 02:46:43.100
You know, and he was just crying through the hall.

02:46:43.100 --> 02:46:46.040
Here's everybody cheering, he's this guy behind me crying.

02:46:46.040 --> 02:46:48.660
Limp as a rag.

02:46:48.660 --> 02:46:51.550
And I had expected there, of course,

02:46:51.550 --> 02:46:54.430
to be moments where they would scream at the shock,

02:46:54.430 --> 02:46:56.530
and I'd expected a lot of nervous muttering

02:46:56.530 --> 02:46:58.530
and tittering, all that stuff.

02:46:58.530 --> 02:46:59.920
Wasn't what happened at all.

02:47:00.670 --> 02:47:05.670
The audience sat in absolute silence, unmoving,

02:47:05.670 --> 02:47:07.730
staring at that screen.

02:47:07.730 --> 02:47:10.450
- Like all of us, we can't be objective about our own work.

02:47:10.450 --> 02:47:12.520
Even when he went to the previews,

02:47:12.520 --> 02:47:13.670
he just wasn't sure.

02:47:13.670 --> 02:47:15.370
He thought, oh, maybe it's just those audiences,

02:47:15.370 --> 02:47:17.690
they're prone to love it.

02:47:17.690 --> 02:47:19.330
And nobody else is gonna like it.

02:47:19.330 --> 02:47:22.220
Maybe not the masses that aren't the science fiction lovers.

02:47:22.220 --> 02:47:24.480
But you could tell that crowd, they went wild.

02:47:24.480 --> 02:47:26.140
I mean, they were just standing and cheering

02:47:26.140 --> 02:47:27.950
at the end of the movie.

02:47:27.950 --> 02:47:29.730
- I saw it at the Egyptian.

02:47:30.860 --> 02:47:34.060
The first time I saw it in its entirety,

02:47:34.060 --> 02:47:36.830
a lot of them had come up and were standing in front.

02:47:36.830 --> 02:47:39.100
They'd walked out on the movie.

02:47:39.100 --> 02:47:42.030
And I thought, cool, it can't be that bad, right?

02:47:42.030 --> 02:47:45.580
So I go in there, watch the movie, right?

02:47:45.580 --> 02:47:48.640
And I'm quite excited by what's happening.

02:47:48.640 --> 02:47:52.090
I thought the pacing cutting was brilliant, right?

02:47:52.090 --> 02:47:57.040
And then when Sigourney goes back to pick up a cat,

02:47:57.040 --> 02:48:00.000
I had this wonderful African-American

02:48:00.030 --> 02:48:04.030
Shut up, "Leave the fucking cat!"

02:48:04.030 --> 02:48:08.030
And at that stage, I knew that Ridley had got a hit on his hands.

02:48:08.030 --> 02:48:12.780
They had that whole space jockey in front.

02:48:12.780 --> 02:48:17.150
I understand somebody tried to set fire to it or something.

02:48:17.150 --> 02:48:21.970
They thought it was the work of the devil. Great.

02:48:21.970 --> 02:48:25.100
By then, we had already had the night before I'd been to the Academy,

02:48:25.100 --> 02:48:28.110
and that's a tougher audience because they're filmmakers,

02:48:28.110 --> 02:48:30.000
and it takes a lot to impress them.

02:48:30.000 --> 02:48:32.690
But that went so amazingly.

02:48:32.690 --> 02:48:38.160
Sitting behind me was Jack Nicholson and Warren Beatty and they kept screaming like two

02:48:38.160 --> 02:48:40.810
little boys at every scare.

02:48:40.810 --> 02:48:43.330
And they came up to Dan and I later and said, "Oh, it's amazing, guys."

02:48:43.330 --> 02:48:45.550
And Dan, "My God, it's Warren Beatty and Jack Nicholson!"

02:48:45.550 --> 02:48:47.710
You know, and they scared the shit out of us.

02:48:47.710 --> 02:48:51.540
So we were just beaming and that's a tougher audience.

